{"parentUuid":"56d3902e-c343-4f16-b7de-211421b041a1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"058888eb-98f5-4241-99df-833cc8ac791c","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YMaJUjTkhmGZVAZj3eWX6c","type":"tool_result","content":"| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md | **Fix 11.** The quote is now \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes ... running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270). It notes that the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF 281 (verified against the image), so the bracketed letters are not printed. **Missing idea 5** is added as a bullet: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances \n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md | **Fix 12.** The sentence is now quoted as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps ...\", followed by \"(sic: the sentence runs across the page break as 'No resistance / methods should not use', PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is 'should not')\". The locator line now says both pages were verified against the page images. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md | **Wilson.** A \"Source note\" is added after the internal-conflict paragraph with the reference: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991), *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 23(3):364-370, with the abstract's design and its \"half-life of 0.85 s\". It says the abstract does not give the 4 s figure. It adds an Inference that 0.85 s matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so this is probably the bench-press study and the caption's 1991 is right. It says the 0.37 s Wilson (1991) result was not checked. `contested` is kept: the book itself calls the decay resu\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Low vs high inertia training effect (PDF 284) | Added to the classification entry (flagged; same page as Fig 5.8). Ch1 `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` has F = ma and \"different training effect\" but not the low-inertia/neural vs high-inertia/hypertrophy split, so the 5b passage is new. |\n| 2 | Strength-endurance specificity is weaker than speed's, carry-over greater (PDF 296) | Added to the ceiling entry (flagged; the sentence sits just before the ceiling quote). The Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry already quotes the Ch3 version, and the new bullet links it. |\n| 3 | Fig 5.9 apparatus catalogue (PDF 285) | Added to the momentum-intensity entry (flagged; Fig 5.9 introduces the same prescription list that entry quotes). |\n| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Wilson citation\n\nThe Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1991;23(3):364-370. Twelve experienced male weight lifters, bench press at 95% of maximum, decay fitted by a negative exponential with a half-life of 0.85 s. That matches \n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n31:| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n32-| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n33-\n34-No new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n35-\n36-## Wilson citation\n37-\n38-The Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle\n39-\n40:## Disagreements with the review\n41-\n42-1. **Missing idea 4 (upper-body transfer) is not missing.** `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` already quotes the PDF 288 / p.277 sentence (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses). The source gives that point in one sentence, and the entry gives it one line. No edit was made. Only \"loaded swings or other specialised apparatus\" is left\n43-2. **Fix 4(3) and fix 5: the review's wording \"order made no significant difference in novices\" is too broad.** PDF 299 holds three relevant findings. (a) Across the preparatory period, order \"has no real significance for novices\". (b) In an across-stage experiment, weights first, then depth jumps, \"was more effective\". (c) In the within-workout experiment, which is the one that matches \"resist\n44-3. **Fix 7(1): the reading is stated as Inference, not fact.** The source sentence is \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 ...\". Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps (confirmed on the image), but \"each exercise\" could grammatically take in the depth jumps. I moved item 1 out of Model 2 as asked, labelled the rea\n45-4. **Fix 7(2): voice labels are per item, not per model.** Model 1 includes prescription item 6 and Model 2 includes prescription item 4. Both come from the PDF 285 list, which the review itself treats as \"the authors'\" (momentum row). So I labelled the Siff passages Siff, the prescription items the authors', and the PDF 286 list \"credited to 'prolific experience' ... whose figures are credited\n46-5. **Fix 10: the full baseline tag is used.** The review gives \"(Inferred, protocol)\". Line 77 prints \"**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**\", and the brief asks for the tag verbatim.\n47-6. **Beyond the review: baseline tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files** (lines 74, 76, 105, 116, 117, 122, 186). Line 105 needed this most: it is Inferred and \"Not claimed in the interview\", and the entry called it \"the stated rule\" with no tag.\n48-7. **Optional items not applied.** The review's optional rows for GO entries (Dursenev wording, \"text layer\" to \"verified\" in three GO entries, the cyclic-endurance lead, the frequency entry's line 186 anchor, the non-impact locator) are outside the \"flagged files only\" rule and were not applied. Exception: the line 186 anchor was added to the volume-and-rest entry, which is flagged; the review\n49-\n50-Every other review claim held up on the page: the PDF 281 margin cut (\"adequ\", \"running a\"), the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break, the Fig 5.11 ground jumps, the absence of \"jumping from depth\" in the p.278 passage, the PDF 233 \"considerably lower\" locator, the baseline lines 74/76/77/116/117, Siff's \"powermetrics\" on PDF 279 and in the Fig 5.8 caption, and the rowers' 50-80% ver\n51-\n52-## For the coordinator","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c7b1a6db-0e29-40e5-a1a7-84307b205e84","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:18:57.930Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md | **Fix 11.** The quote is now \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes ... running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270). It notes that the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF 281 (verified against the image), so the bracketed letters are not printed. **Missing idea 5** is added as a bullet: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances \n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md | **Fix 12.** The sentence is now quoted as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps ...\", followed by \"(sic: the sentence runs across the page break as 'No resistance / methods should not use', PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is 'should not')\". The locator line now says both pages were verified against the page images. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md | **Wilson.** A \"Source note\" is added after the internal-conflict paragraph with the reference: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991), *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 23(3):364-370, with the abstract's design and its \"half-life of 0.85 s\". It says the abstract does not give the 4 s figure. It adds an Inference that 0.85 s matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so this is probably the bench-press study and the caption's 1991 is right. It says the 0.37 s Wilson (1991) result was not checked. `contested` is kept: the book itself calls the decay resu\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Low vs high inertia training effect (PDF 284) | Added to the classification entry (flagged; same page as Fig 5.8). Ch1 `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` has F = ma and \"different training effect\" but not the low-inertia/neural vs high-inertia/hypertrophy split, so the 5b passage is new. |\n| 2 | Strength-endurance specificity is weaker than speed's, carry-over greater (PDF 296) | Added to the ceiling entry (flagged; the sentence sits just before the ceiling quote). The Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry already quotes the Ch3 version, and the new bullet links it. |\n| 3 | Fig 5.9 apparatus catalogue (PDF 285) | Added to the momentum-intensity entry (flagged; Fig 5.9 introduces the same prescription list that entry quotes). |\n| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Wilson citation\n\nThe Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1991;23(3):364-370. Twelve experienced male weight lifters, bench press at 95% of maximum, decay fitted by a negative exponential with a half-life of 0.85 s. That matches \n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n31:| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n32-| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n33-\n34-No new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n35-\n36-## Wilson citation\n37-\n38-The Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle\n39-\n40:## Disagreements with the review\n41-\n42-1. **Missing idea 4 (upper-body transfer) is not missing.** `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` already quotes the PDF 288 / p.277 sentence (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses). The source gives that point in one sentence, and the entry gives it one line. No edit was made. Only \"loaded swings or other specialised apparatus\" is left\n43-2. **Fix 4(3) and fix 5: the review's wording \"order made no significant difference in novices\" is too broad.** PDF 299 holds three relevant findings. (a) Across the preparatory period, order \"has no real significance for novices\". (b) In an across-stage experiment, weights first, then depth jumps, \"was more effective\". (c) In the within-workout experiment, which is the one that matches \"resist\n44-3. **Fix 7(1): the reading is stated as Inference, not fact.** The source sentence is \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 ...\". Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps (confirmed on the image), but \"each exercise\" could grammatically take in the depth jumps. I moved item 1 out of Model 2 as asked, labelled the rea\n45-4. **Fix 7(2): voice labels are per item, not per model.** Model 1 includes prescription item 6 and Model 2 includes prescription item 4. Both come from the PDF 285 list, which the review itself treats as \"the authors'\" (momentum row). So I labelled the Siff passages Siff, the prescription items the authors', and the PDF 286 list \"credited to 'prolific experience' ... whose figures are credited\n46-5. **Fix 10: the full baseline tag is used.** The review gives \"(Inferred, protocol)\". Line 77 prints \"**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**\", and the brief asks for the tag verbatim.\n47-6. **Beyond the review: baseline tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files** (lines 74, 76, 105, 116, 117, 122, 186). Line 105 needed this most: it is Inferred and \"Not claimed in the interview\", and the entry called it \"the stated rule\" with no tag.\n48-7. **Optional items not applied.** The review's optional rows for GO entries (Dursenev wording, \"text layer\" to \"verified\" in three GO entries, the cyclic-endurance lead, the frequency entry's line 186 anchor, the non-impact locator) are outside the \"flagged files only\" rule and were not applied. Exception: the line 186 anchor was added to the volume-and-rest entry, which is flagged; the review\n49-\n50-Every other review claim held up on the page: the PDF 281 margin cut (\"adequ\", \"running a\"), the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break, the Fig 5.11 ground jumps, the absence of \"jumping from depth\" in the p.278 passage, the PDF 233 \"considerably lower\" locator, the baseline lines 74/76/77/116/117, Siff's \"powermetrics\" on PDF 279 and in the Fig 5.8 caption, and the rowers' 50-80% ver\n51-\n52-## For the coordinator","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"56d3902e-c343-4f16-b7de-211421b041a1","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"362a7d79-8785-49b5-937b-9ad410420e66","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"058888eb-98f5-4241-99df-833cc8ac791c","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LRgSDZcJNuVQ1Ryy3tA6ZP","type":"tool_result","content":"# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"48d5fb79-2840-42f0-9963-582b115f116b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:18:58.177Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Atomization brief (shared by every Flint ingest agent)\n\nRead this whole file before writing anything. It holds the rules the Harries review taught (2026-09-26: 25 of 35 files needed fixes). The review gate will check every rule here.\n\n## Schema\n\nFollow `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\.claude\\skills\\ingest-book\\SKILL.md` exactly: `_domain.md`, `_book.md` (map and resume checkpoint, updated after EACH chapter or unit), and entries named `<kind>--<kebab-slug>.md` with kind one of principle, framework, case-study, heuristic, claim. Use the frontmatter keys exactly as the skill gives them. The body has **What it is** / **Why it works** / **When to apply** / **When NOT to apply** (mandatory, non-trivial) / **Example** / **Related**.\n\nStyle reference, read before writing: `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library\\strength-and-conditioning\\fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer\\case-study--isometric-sprinter-and-hurdler.md`, plus one principle file in the same folder.\n\n## Voices: label every one\n\n- **The source author's statements are plain text.**\n- **Eric's views** (from `docs/method-baseline.md` or his reviews) are prefixed \"Eric's baseline:\" or \"Eric's review:\".\n- **Your own reasoning, application or synthesis** is prefixed \"Inference:\".\n- **Nothing unlabeled that the author did not say.** The most common failures: a judgment from Eric's review written as if the author said it; a mechanism the author never gave presented as \"why it works\" (write \"The author gives no mechanism. Inference: ...\"); an invented example.\n\n## Lessons from the Supertraining Chapter 1 review (2026-09-26: 30 FIX, 1 NO-GO of 41)\n\n- **Keep the source's internal contradictions.** When a book says X on one page and not-X on another, record both with their pages and set `contested`. Never pick one side silently. The NO-GO entry reversed the book's SJ-CMJ strength-deficit rule because the book states it both ways.\n- **Name the right voice.** In co-authored books, write \"the authors\" unless a passage clearly belongs to one author. A passage that names Verkhoshansky in the third person is Siff writing.\n- **Keep `proven` rare and scoped.** Scope it to the replicated core (for example, \"Hill's force-velocity curve\"), never the whole entry, and mark any disputed sentence inside it. Four of eight `proven` ratings were lowered.\n- **No mechanism the source does not give.** About a dozen \"Why it works\" sections supplied physiology the book never states. Label it \"Inference:\" or leave it out.\n- **Do not add \"well established\" or \"modern research shows\" without a citation.** Name the paper, or delete the phrase.\n\n## Lessons from the 2026-09-27 review wave (Supertraining Ch2-3, Issurin Ch3, Bondarchuk, Evans, Old Bull)\n\n- **Invented Examples are the most common failure.** 34 of 36 Supertraining Ch2-3a Examples were unlabelled inventions. Use the source's own example where it gives one; otherwise start the Example with \"Inference:\".\n- **\"Verified against the page image\" means you opened the image.** Four Issurin Ch3 entries claimed it for figures they had misread (Fig 3.7 inverted, a Table 3.7 row swapped). Sparse slides are not \"bare diagrams\" until you have looked.\n- **Name the population and method the source gives.** Bondarchuk's data are 7,000+ native and foreign athletes, questionnaires, r >= 0.349, steroid-free; entries that said \"Soviet athletes\" were wrong everywhere.\n- **Check what a cited paper actually says.** Issurin 2016's critique targets Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, not Issurin's A-T-R; Herzog & Leonard 2000 shows force enhancement, against the book's sentence. When the book misreports a study, record both.\n- **No review history in entries.** \"Prior version\", \"corrected from a NO-GO finding\" and similar belong in `staging/fixes_*_applied.md`, never in the entry body.\n- **Fix logs must match the files.** One fixer log claimed fixes that were not made. Fixers run their own grep checks and paste the output.\n- **Section headers use the vault form** `**What it is** —` (with the em dash). Do not add suffixes such as \": mandatory\". Em dashes are banned only in titles and body text. Never wrap a `[[wikilink]]` across lines.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Every number, definition and quote carries a checkable locator: PDF page (and printed page if it differs), or file plus timestamp. Never write \"confirmed in PDF\" unless you opened that page.\n- Never invent a number. Keep ambiguity visible: mark unclear audio or OCR as unclear, and do not harmonize two conflicting numbers from the source; record both with their locators.\n- OCR sources (`staging/ocr/*.md`): pages listed as low_confidence in the header must be checked against the page image before any number from them is cited. Use the Read tool on the PDF page, or render it.\n- **OCR confidence scores miss the worst errors** (checked 2026-09-27). Zatsiorsky 2e: the OCR interleaves the two columns and the sidebars line by line, so its text is not readable prose; use it only to find content, and read and quote from the page image. *CFTS*: single-column prose that mostly reads in order, but upside-down lines come out as gibberish, some lines are dropped, and figures are scrambled, all at \"0 low-confidence pages\". For any OCR source, quote only after checking the page image, and read every figure and table from the image.\n\n## Confidence\n\n- Practitioner courses and lectures: ceiling `supported`.\n- Books: `proven` only for established, widely replicated physiology or mechanics. Soviet-era or single-lab empirical findings and training prescriptions: `supported` at most. `untested` for single cases and anecdotes. `contested` where another library source or modern evidence disagrees (say which).\n\n## Dedup and converge\n\nBefore each entry, grep `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\SecondBrain\\Knowledge\\library` and the staging library (`C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\staging\\library`) for the concept. If it is covered, add a new entry only for a genuinely new angle, and link the existing one in Related. List reciprocal-link suggestions in your final report. Never edit files in the vault or another agent's staging folder.\n\n## Baylor context\n\nRead `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\Flint\\.claude\\worktrees\\step1-triage\\docs\\method-baseline.md` (approved). Use it in When to apply, labelled \"Eric's baseline:\". Department names: Athletic Performance, Athletic Medicine, Applied Performance. Plain style, no em dashes. Use commas, colons and parentheses.\n\n## Scope and output\n\n- Write ONLY into your assigned staging folder. No git, no vault edits.\n- Stop at a chapter or unit boundary. It is better to finish fewer chapters well; the `_book.md` checkpoint lets the next batch resume.\n- Final reply: entries by kind and by confidence; chapters or units done and pending with page ranges; reciprocal-link suggestions; unclear or contradictory spots in the source; anything you could not read.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"362a7d79-8785-49b5-937b-9ad410420e66","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
