ation).\n639:Adduction: Pcctoralis major, teres major, latissimus dorsi, coracobrachialis, subscapularis, and the long head of \n641:Lateral rotation: Teres minor, infraspinatus, posterior deltoid.\n653:=== PDF 429 ===\n657:Note: Apparent rotation of the elbow joint is actually rotation at the wrist - the radius and ulna twist about one another, so there is no \n680:back which is involved in spinal rotation is multifidus. The major initiators of trunk rotation are the internal and \n685:flexion so as to augment action by rectus abdominis, so it is incorrect to state that trunk flexion is solely a result \n696:different training methods and techniques, while physiotherapists and other medical personnel separately \n698:approaches and their individual multitudes of training methods. Therefore, the author was invited to address the \n701:strength training methods (Siff, 1989). Until that time, PNF was generally regarded as a special type of stretching \n703:for all aspects of musculoskeletal conditioning in the competitive athlete, nor did many physiotherapists know \n707:=== PDF 430 ===\n709:An overview of this presentation and workshop acted as the basis for Chapter 7.2 above, which should \n718:cheating to compel stronger musclcs to help the weaker ones, optimal timing, use of full-range specific patterns of \n719:diagonal-spiral motions (details in Knott & Voss, 1977), application of maximal resistance throughout the non- \n727:effectiveness would increase markedly if the latter were also to be used statically and dynamically over a full range \n761:=== PDF 431 ===","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f3850162-102a-4a93-a9cc-aa2c57e54f06","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
