{"parentUuid":"0b26bddb-33fc-43bd-bb75-31a8cb80c8bc","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffqtmgs69MrpH3ERy8Qn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RBieCoSm33ZMTAes1FM33u","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && grep -n \"430\" claim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md principle--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces.md claim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md | cut -c1-900"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7796,"cache_read_input_tokens":86510,"output_tokens":856,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":621},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7796},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":856,"cache_read_input_tokens":86510,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7796,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7796,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01RBieCoSm33ZMTAes1FM33u":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && grep -n \"430\" claim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md principle--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces.md claim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md | cut -c1-900"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqtmREurppa2RuWHqM4","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5a24a817-4d62-4af1-a1e2-4a36b2068714","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:17.644Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5a24a817-4d62-4af1-a1e2-4a36b2068714","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"058888eb-98f5-4241-99df-833cc8ac791c","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RBieCoSm33ZMTAes1FM33u","type":"tool_result","content":"claim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md:16:**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) writes that PNF methods \"were formulated from findings on neuromuscular development, such as the functional evolution of all movement from motor immaturity to motor maturity in the growing child or novice athlete,\" progressing \"total to individuated; proximal to distal, distal to proximal; mobile to stabile; gross to selective; reflexive to deliberate; overlapping to integrative; incoordinate to coordinate\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405, text layer). In 7.2.3.3 he restates the emphasis for \"the patient, the novice or the child\": \"progression from primitive to complex, gross to individuated, mobile to stabile, reflexive to deliberative, proximal to distal, and incoordinate to coordinate.\" Then: \"Contrary to common belief, the novice \nclaim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md:16:**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) proposes replacing \"modified PNF\" with Functional Neuromuscular Conditioning (FNC), for five reasons: it is \"sometimes desirable to deviate from strict PNF principles\"; other disciplines (\"Feldenkrais, Alexander, yoga, Tai Chi and Laban\") add methods; contraction \"may be facilitated by local after-discharge of the same muscle,\" so not all is neuromuscular; some PNF methods \"may not be classified accurately as proprioceptive, such as cognitive, perceptual and other sensory mechanisms\"; and the full name \"is too technically daunting for the average coach or athlete.\" FNC \"should be regarded as an entire conditioning system capable of developing any desired type of musculoskeletal fitness.\" He then turns to the fashi\nheuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md:16:**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) ranks equipment for modified PNF: \"Pulleys and dumbbells are especially suitable for application of the spiral and diagonal, midline-crossing PNF techniques. ... Barbell training provides fewer opportunities for the use of PNF training, while machines offer the fewest possibilities of all\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410, checked against the page image). Patterns are \"Probably the most neglected aspect of PNF by coaches\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer). \"No machines other than the traditional high-low pulley machines allow the user to produce PNF patterns,\" and \"pec-decks, back hyperextension machines, seated pressing machines, hack-squat machines and leg extension machines ... cause such large deviations from recommen\nheuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md:24:**Example** — Siff's examples: \"The standing full-range cable cross-over exercise with external-internal rotation of the shoulder joint furnishes just one example of an adapted PNF pattern\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410); and in 7.5, for a baseball pitcher or javelin thrower, cable-crossovers \"in a specific spiral-diagonal pattern ... that cross the sagittal midline of the body\" instead of the pec-deck (PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419, checked against the page image). Fig 7.8's caption adds that the trunk rotation pattern can be done supine or standing and \"Resistance may be offered with a cable machine\" (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image).\nprinciple--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces.md:16:**What it is** — Under \"Pattern Deviations and Safety Factors,\" Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) writes that PNF patterns \"were devised for rehabilitation, not elite sporting training, so that it is not incorrect to employ patterns of movement which may deviate significantly from those stipulated in PNF\" (italics in the original). PNF keeps patterns \"close to the body, a situation which occurs seldom in sport,\" where \"forceful or rapid movements have to be executed with the distal extremities far from the relevant joints, thereby imposing large torque on these joints. If training always ensures that this degree of torque or force is avoided, then it will not prepare the athlete for the rigors of the sport and will increase susceptibility to overtraining and injury.\" Hence: \"As in en","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"aee8ff1a-da1f-422e-bfd8-8025143d7152","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:17.846Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"claim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md:16:**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) writes that PNF methods \"were formulated from findings on neuromuscular development, such as the functional evolution of all movement from motor immaturity to motor maturity in the growing child or novice athlete,\" progressing \"total to individuated; proximal to distal, distal to proximal; mobile to stabile; gross to selective; reflexive to deliberate; overlapping to integrative; incoordinate to coordinate\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405, text layer). In 7.2.3.3 he restates the emphasis for \"the patient, the novice or the child\": \"progression from primitive to complex, gross to individuated, mobile to stabile, reflexive to deliberative, proximal to distal, and incoordinate to coordinate.\" Then: \"Contrary to common belief, the novice \nclaim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md:16:**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) proposes replacing \"modified PNF\" with Functional Neuromuscular Conditioning (FNC), for five reasons: it is \"sometimes desirable to deviate from strict PNF principles\"; other disciplines (\"Feldenkrais, Alexander, yoga, Tai Chi and Laban\") add methods; contraction \"may be facilitated by local after-discharge of the same muscle,\" so not all is neuromuscular; some PNF methods \"may not be classified accurately as proprioceptive, such as cognitive, perceptual and other sensory mechanisms\"; and the full name \"is too technically daunting for the average coach or athlete.\" FNC \"should be regarded as an entire conditioning system capable of developing any desired type of musculoskeletal fitness.\" He then turns to the fashi\nheuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md:16:**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) ranks equipment for modified PNF: \"Pulleys and dumbbells are especially suitable for application of the spiral and diagonal, midline-crossing PNF techniques. ... Barbell training provides fewer opportunities for the use of PNF training, while machines offer the fewest possibilities of all\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410, checked against the page image). Patterns are \"Probably the most neglected aspect of PNF by coaches\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer). \"No machines other than the traditional high-low pulley machines allow the user to produce PNF patterns,\" and \"pec-decks, back hyperextension machines, seated pressing machines, hack-squat machines and leg extension machines ... cause such large deviations from recommen\nheuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md:24:**Example** — Siff's examples: \"The standing full-range cable cross-over exercise with external-internal rotation of the shoulder joint furnishes just one example of an adapted PNF pattern\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410); and in 7.5, for a baseball pitcher or javelin thrower, cable-crossovers \"in a specific spiral-diagonal pattern ... that cross the sagittal midline of the body\" instead of the pec-deck (PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419, checked against the page image). Fig 7.8's caption adds that the trunk rotation pattern can be done supine or standing and \"Resistance may be offered with a cable machine\" (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image).\nprinciple--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces.md:16:**What it is** — Under \"Pattern Deviations and Safety Factors,\" Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) writes that PNF patterns \"were devised for rehabilitation, not elite sporting training, so that it is not incorrect to employ patterns of movement which may deviate significantly from those stipulated in PNF\" (italics in the original). PNF keeps patterns \"close to the body, a situation which occurs seldom in sport,\" where \"forceful or rapid movements have to be executed with the distal extremities far from the relevant joints, thereby imposing large torque on these joints. If training always ensures that this degree of torque or force is avoided, then it will not prepare the athlete for the rigors of the sport and will increase susceptibility to overtraining and injury.\" Hence: \"As in en","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5a24a817-4d62-4af1-a1e2-4a36b2068714","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
