{"parentUuid":"2a631494-b7a5-4355-be2d-7acfb151748a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffqw2NxD2MWDhn6fR6yJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DD7p8HcxNDC68nAXyAU996","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && cat -n case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7746,"cache_read_input_tokens":112981,"output_tokens":387,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":228},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7746},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":387,"cache_read_input_tokens":112981,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7746,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7746,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DD7p8HcxNDC68nAXyAU996":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && cat -n case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqvwVHLpR9KacKVx7jN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"85542de1-4851-48b6-8fda-fe43725899ee","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:44.737Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"85542de1-4851-48b6-8fda-fe43725899ee","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"058888eb-98f5-4241-99df-833cc8ac791c","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DD7p8HcxNDC68nAXyAU996","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: case-study\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.3\"\n     7\ttopic: combined free weight and isokinetic resistance\n     8\tcategory: [variable-resistance, isokinetic, power, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Fradd (1995): Elbow Flexion Under Free Weight, Isokinetic and Combined Resistance\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors report a study in which \"the same group of subjects\" did elbow flexion under free-weight (isoinertial), isokinetic, and combined resistance, the combination applied \"using a cable machine specially designed and constructed for this purpose by one of Siff's mechanical engineering students at the University of the Witwatersrand in South Africa (Fradd, 1995)\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image). Number of subjects, their training status, loads and isokinetic speed are not given. Table 7.1 (velocity in m/s, power in W, force in N):\n    17\t\n    18\t| Resistance | Max velocity | Mean power | Peak power | Mean force | Peak force |\n    19\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    20\t| Isoinertial | 1.49 | 151 | 306 | 184 | 219 |\n    21\t| Isokinetic | 0.42 | 80 | 129 | 247 | 350 |\n    22\t| Combined | 0.38 | 102 | 131 | 336 | 380 |\n    23\t\n    24\tThe five stated findings: (1) \"Greatest mean and peak power is produced under free weight conditions, reaching a peak approximately midway through the movement\"; (2) \"Free weight force production is greatest during the earliest phases of joint movement\"; (3) isokinetic and combined power is \"considerably lower\" but \"remains near its peak value for a considerable part of the range\"; (4) \"Mean and peak force production is greatest during combined conditions, and may peak twice during the movement, most commonly near the beginning and the end of range\"; (5) \"Isokinetic force plateaus about one-third through the range, but commonly increases near end of range\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). For Fig 7.10 the text adds that peak power and velocity come \"about halfway through the movement, when elbow angle is approximately 90 degrees,\" that \"Approximately half the movement is spent in acceleration and half in deceleration,\" and that free-weight peak power is \"some three times greater than under isokinetic or combined auxotonic-isokinetic conditions\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413, checked against the page image).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the differences beyond the resistance types themselves. They stress variability: curve shape \"varies with every individual, according to their motor characteristics, and, in the combined scenario, with the magnitude of the inertial load and the velocity of isokinetic resistance\"; combined resistance \"may be made more auxotonic (free weight) if the added weight is large and more isokinetic if the added load is relatively small\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). Inference: free weight lets the load accelerate and then forces deceleration, which yields high peak power mid-range; an isokinetic device caps velocity, so force can stay high while power stays low.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)).\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424). (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The study itself: in the combined condition, mean force was 336 N against 184 N under free weight, while peak power was 131 W against 306 W (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (the second hybrid in 7.3), [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] (4.2.6.1), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (the authors' free-weight alternative, named in the 7.3 opening), [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]] (mechanics of constant, isokinetic and variable resistance)\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: case-study\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.3\"\n     7\ttopic: bands and chains with free weights\n     8\tcategory: [variable-resistance, accommodating-resistance, eccentric, powerlifting]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Box Squat With Free Weights Plus Elastic Bands: Greater Force Through the Range and a Faster Descent\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors describe a box squat in a power rack \"with strong elastic bands attached between the ends of the bar and the base of the rack,\" band length set so resistance \"varied between set limits at the lowest and the highest positions\" (PDF pp. 424-425 / printed pp. 413-414, checked against the page images). The powerlifters (number not given) had \"in addition to the squat weight of 200kg, an added elastic resistance of 150kg at the lowest position while sitting down on the box with knees at approximately right angles and a greatest elastic resistance of 250kg at the top of the squat with knees fully extended\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The stated differences from free-weight training: \"1. A greater mean and peak force were produced throughout the range of movement. 2. The descent onto the box tended to be accelerated above the normal gravitational rate of 9.8m/sec squared, so that greater eccentric force had to be generated to control the downward motion. 3. The stronger eccentric loading and the brief transition period involved while sitting before exploding upwards provided neuromuscular stimulation which approximates that usually encountered in popular plyometric training. 4. The force generated during the later stages increased, in strong contrast to the situation of normal squatting in which force production tends to decrease significantly\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Fig 7.13 (checked against the page image) plots force as a percentage of the bar weight against time: the elastic component runs from about 125% at the top to 75% at the bottom (150/200 and 250/200, matching the text); combined resistance runs from about 225% at the top to about 175% at the bottom and back to about 225%; the eccentric phase spans about 0 to 2.3 s, the box transition about 2.3 to 3.1 s, and the concentric phase about 3.1 to 6 s. The force attributed to the weight itself dips to about 90% during the descent and rises to about 120% early in the ascent.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is items 2 and 3 above: band recoil accelerates the descent beyond gravity, demanding more eccentric force, and the short pause on the box before driving up resembles plyometric loading (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Item 4 follows from the band tension rising as the lifter stands. The 7.3 opening gives the general reason for variable resistance: force capacity \"changes with joint angle,\" so \"any given load will tend to underload certain muscles over some part of the movement range\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image).\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5). The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own: 200 kg on the bar, bands adding 150 kg on the box and 250 kg at lockout, so the lifter meets about 350 kg at the bottom and about 450 kg at the top (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414; the totals are this entry's arithmetic from the stated values, and match Fig 7.13's 175% and 225%).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion]], [[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]], [[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]], [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]], [[research-papers/rhea-2009-alterations-in-speed-of-squat-movement-and-the-use-of-accommodated-resistance-among-colleg]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"1eae3d82-801f-411a-af5b-70fbc4331abe","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:44.917Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: case-study\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.3\"\n     7\ttopic: combined free weight and isokinetic resistance\n     8\tcategory: [variable-resistance, isokinetic, power, biomechanics]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Fradd (1995): Elbow Flexion Under Free Weight, Isokinetic and Combined Resistance\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors report a study in which \"the same group of subjects\" did elbow flexion under free-weight (isoinertial), isokinetic, and combined resistance, the combination applied \"using a cable machine specially designed and constructed for this purpose by one of Siff's mechanical engineering students at the University of the Witwatersrand in South Africa (Fradd, 1995)\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image). Number of subjects, their training status, loads and isokinetic speed are not given. Table 7.1 (velocity in m/s, power in W, force in N):\n    17\t\n    18\t| Resistance | Max velocity | Mean power | Peak power | Mean force | Peak force |\n    19\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    20\t| Isoinertial | 1.49 | 151 | 306 | 184 | 219 |\n    21\t| Isokinetic | 0.42 | 80 | 129 | 247 | 350 |\n    22\t| Combined | 0.38 | 102 | 131 | 336 | 380 |\n    23\t\n    24\tThe five stated findings: (1) \"Greatest mean and peak power is produced under free weight conditions, reaching a peak approximately midway through the movement\"; (2) \"Free weight force production is greatest during the earliest phases of joint movement\"; (3) isokinetic and combined power is \"considerably lower\" but \"remains near its peak value for a considerable part of the range\"; (4) \"Mean and peak force production is greatest during combined conditions, and may peak twice during the movement, most commonly near the beginning and the end of range\"; (5) \"Isokinetic force plateaus about one-third through the range, but commonly increases near end of range\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). For Fig 7.10 the text adds that peak power and velocity come \"about halfway through the movement, when elbow angle is approximately 90 degrees,\" that \"Approximately half the movement is spent in acceleration and half in deceleration,\" and that free-weight peak power is \"some three times greater than under isokinetic or combined auxotonic-isokinetic conditions\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413, checked against the page image).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the differences beyond the resistance types themselves. They stress variability: curve shape \"varies with every individual, according to their motor characteristics, and, in the combined scenario, with the magnitude of the inertial load and the velocity of isokinetic resistance\"; combined resistance \"may be made more auxotonic (free weight) if the added weight is large and more isokinetic if the added load is relatively small\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). Inference: free weight lets the load accelerate and then forces deceleration, which yields high peak power mid-range; an isokinetic device caps velocity, so force can stay high while power stays low.\n    27\t\n    28\t**When to apply** — When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)).\n    29\t\n    30\t**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424). (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s.\n    31\t\n    32\t**Example** — The study itself: in the combined condition, mean force was 336 N against 184 N under free weight, while peak power was 131 W against 306 W (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image).\n    33\t\n    34\t**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (the second hybrid in 7.3), [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] (4.2.6.1), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (the authors' free-weight alternative, named in the 7.3 opening), [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]] (mechanics of constant, isokinetic and variable resistance)\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: case-study\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.3\"\n     7\ttopic: bands and chains with free weights\n     8\tcategory: [variable-resistance, accommodating-resistance, eccentric, powerlifting]\n     9\tconfidence: contested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Box Squat With Free Weights Plus Elastic Bands: Greater Force Through the Range and a Faster Descent\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The authors describe a box squat in a power rack \"with strong elastic bands attached between the ends of the bar and the base of the rack,\" band length set so resistance \"varied between set limits at the lowest and the highest positions\" (PDF pp. 424-425 / printed pp. 413-414, checked against the page images). The powerlifters (number not given) had \"in addition to the squat weight of 200kg, an added elastic resistance of 150kg at the lowest position while sitting down on the box with knees at approximately right angles and a greatest elastic resistance of 250kg at the top of the squat with knees fully extended\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The stated differences from free-weight training: \"1. A greater mean and peak force were produced throughout the range of movement. 2. The descent onto the box tended to be accelerated above the normal gravitational rate of 9.8m/sec squared, so that greater eccentric force had to be generated to control the downward motion. 3. The stronger eccentric loading and the brief transition period involved while sitting before exploding upwards provided neuromuscular stimulation which approximates that usually encountered in popular plyometric training. 4. The force generated during the later stages increased, in strong contrast to the situation of normal squatting in which force production tends to decrease significantly\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Fig 7.13 (checked against the page image) plots force as a percentage of the bar weight against time: the elastic component runs from about 125% at the top to 75% at the bottom (150/200 and 250/200, matching the text); combined resistance runs from about 225% at the top to about 175% at the bottom and back to about 225%; the eccentric phase spans about 0 to 2.3 s, the box transition about 2.3 to 3.1 s, and the concentric phase about 3.1 to 6 s. The force attributed to the weight itself dips to about 90% during the descent and rises to about 120% early in the ascent.\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is items 2 and 3 above: band recoil accelerates the descent beyond gravity, demanding more eccentric force, and the short pause on the box before driving up resembles plyometric loading (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Item 4 follows from the band tension rising as the lifter stands. The 7.3 opening gives the general reason for variable resistance: force capacity \"changes with joint angle,\" so \"any given load will tend to underload certain muscles over some part of the movement range\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image).\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it.\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5). The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own: 200 kg on the bar, bands adding 150 kg on the box and 250 kg at lockout, so the lifter meets about 350 kg at the bottom and about 450 kg at the top (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414; the totals are this entry's arithmetic from the stated values, and match Fig 7.13's 175% and 225%).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion]], [[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]], [[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]], [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]], [[research-papers/rhea-2009-alterations-in-speed-of-squat-movement-and-the-use-of-accommodated-resistance-among-colleg]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"85542de1-4851-48b6-8fda-fe43725899ee","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
