ts\\|47\\|63\\|untrained\" jandacka*.md | cut -c1-300"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffqwfB2rbzwRcHr9PsQb","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"de9d8a6a-a962-48a6-bdb3-ce034672f966","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:58.561Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"de9d8a6a-a962-48a6-bdb3-ce034672f966","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012kpe4mQv8n3ihhgLRGJUgN","type":"tool_result","content":"frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance.md\njandacka-2008-a-regression-model-to-determine-load-for-maximum-power-output.md\nrhea-2009-alterations-in-speed-of-squat-movement-and-the-use-of-accommodated-resistance-among-colleg.md\n13:tags: [\"resistance-training\", \"ballistic-training\", \"variable-resistance\", \"bands\", \"pneumatic-resistance\", \"power\"]\n26:This review provides a mechanical framework for understanding why constant, ballistic, accommodating, and variable resistance produce different force, velocity, power, and muscle-activity profiles.\n35:- Covers constant free-weight resistance, stretch-shortening-cycle actions, ballistic movements, hydraulic and isokinetic devices, cams and levers, bands and chains, and pneumatic resistance.\n36:- The applied context is transfer from gym-based strength and power work to sport performance.\n48:- The review concludes that constant resistance creates substantial phase-dependent variation in kinetics and kinematics, while ballistic execution reduces deceleration constraints but does not solve every power-development limitation. [p. 21]\n49:- Bands may sustain acceleration and late-phase force or velocity, but the review states that improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights. [p. 21]\n50:- Pneumatic resistance has plausible mechanical advantages through lower moving mass, but the hypothesized benefit for sport-specific velocity and power remained untested. [p. 21]\n56:- Several proposed benefits of bands and pneumatic resistance were theoretical or unsupported by direct transfer studies at publication.\n57:- Additional transfer limitation (practitioner interpretation): a resistance device cannot be selected from its label alone; exercise, load, intent, range, and athlete status determine the actual stimulus.\n61:Start with the adaptation and movement constraint required, then choose a resistance mode that creates the intended force and velocity profile. Ballistic, banded, or pneumatic methods can complement free weights, but the evidence does not justify declaring one mode universally superior; mixed strategies and direct outcome monitoring are more defensible.\n69:| Resistance-mode conclusions and evidence gaps | p. 21 | conclusion |\n76:- [[strength-power-transfer]]\n----\n12:domain: [\"strength-and-conditioning\", \"power-development\"]\n13:tags: [\"accommodating-resistance\", \"elastic-bands\", \"squat-velocity\", \"power\", \"college-athletes\"]\n19:# Rhea et al. 2009 — Squat speed, bands, and lower-body power\n22:> Rhea, M. R., Kenn, J. G., & Dermody, B. M. (2009). Alterations in speed of squat movement and the use of accommodated resistance among college athletes training for power. *Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, 23*(9). https://doi.org/10.1519/JSC.0b013e3181b3e1b6\n26:This intervention compares slow squatting, fast squatting, and fast squatting with elastic-band accommodating resistance in collegiate athletes.\n30:Do squat movement speed and accommodating resistance differentially affect jump power and squat strength?\n40:- Twelve-week randomized group intervention after three weeks of familiarization.\n41:- Groups used slow squats, fast squats, or fast squats plus elastic bands.\n46:- Jump power improved 17.8% in the fast-plus-bands group, 11.0% with fast squats, and 4.8% with slow squats. [p. 4]\n47:- Fast-plus-bands exceeded slow training for power improvement (p = .02); its difference from fast-only did not reach .05 (p = .09). [p. 5]\n48:- Squat-strength changes did not differ significantly among groups. [p. 5]\n49:- Fast-plus-bands and slow training had similar descriptive strength gains, about 9.4–9.6%. [p. 5]\n53:- Statistical power averaged only .67. [p. 4]\n56:- Band tension and bar-speed prescriptions require equipment-specific replication.\n60:Intent and velocity matter for power development, but the study does not prove bands are superior to every fast-load strategy. Use bands when they help preserve acceleration through the range and verify bar-speed and jump response.\n66:| Participants and grouping | p. 3 | method |\n67:| Power changes | p. 4 | result |\n68:| Between-group tests and strength | p. 5 | result and limitation |\n---\n11:doi: \"10.1080/14763140802266934\"\n22:> Jandacka, D., & Vaverka, F. (2008). A regression model to determine load for maximum power output. *Sports Biomechanics, 7*(3), 361–371. https://doi.org/10.1080/14763140802266934\n34:- Fifty-five male and 48 female physical-education students.\n48:- For the acceleration phase, estimated optimal bench-press loads were 56 ± 9% 1RM in men and 63 ± 8% 1RM in women. [p. 11]\n49:- Corresponding countermovement-squat loads were 47 ± 9% of maximum dynamic strength in men and 54 ± 9% in women. [p. 11]\n55:- The sample comprised young physical-education students rather than sport-specific elite athletes.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3d8969d6-b208-4b5c-b3a6-7ceca2711e7e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:58.813Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance.md\njandacka-2008-a-regression-model-to-determine-load-for-maximum-power-output.md\nrhea-2009-alterations-in-speed-of-squat-movement-and-the-use-of-accommodated-resistance-among-colleg.md\n13:tags: [\"resistance-training\", \"ballistic-training\", \"variable-resistance\", \"bands\", \"pneumatic-resistance\", \"power\"]\n26:This review provides a mechanical framework for understanding why constant, ballistic, accommodating, and variable resistance produce different force, velocity, power, and muscle-activity profiles.\n35:- Covers constant free-weight resistance, stretch-shortening-cycle actions, ballistic movements, hydraulic and isokinetic devices, cams and levers, bands and chains, and pneumatic resistance.\n36:- The applied context is transfer from gym-based strength and power work to sport performance.\n48:- The review concludes that constant resistance creates substantial phase-dependent variation in kinetics and kinematics, while ballistic execution reduces deceleration constraints but does not solve every power-development limitation. [p. 21]\n49:- Bands may sustain acceleration and late-phase force or velocity, but the review states that improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights. [p. 21]\n50:- Pneumatic resistance has plausible mechanical advantages through lower moving mass, but the hypothesized benefit for sport-specific velocity and power remained untested. [p. 21]\n56:- Several proposed benefits of bands and pneumatic resistance were theoretical or unsupported by direct transfer studies at publication.\n57:- Additional transfer limitation (practitioner interpretation): a resistance device cannot be selected from its label alone; exercise, load, intent, range, and athlete status determine the actual stimulus.\n61:Start with the adaptation and movement constraint required, then choose a resistance mode that creates the intended force and velocity profile. Ballistic, banded, or pneumatic methods can complement free weights, but the evidence does not justify declaring one mode universally superior; mixed strategies and direct outcome monitoring are more defensible.\n69:| Resistance-mode conclusions and evidence gaps | p. 21 | conclusion |\n76:- [[strength-power-transfer]]\n----\n12:domain: [\"strength-and-conditioning\", \"power-development\"]\n13:tags: [\"accommodating-resistance\", \"elastic-bands\", \"squat-velocity\", \"power\", \"college-athletes\"]\n19:# Rhea et al. 2009 — Squat speed, bands, and lower-body power\n22:> Rhea, M. R., Kenn, J. G., & Dermody, B. M. (2009). Alterations in speed of squat movement and the use of accommodated resistance among college athletes training for power. *Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research, 23*(9). https://doi.org/10.1519/JSC.0b013e3181b3e1b6\n26:This intervention compares slow squatting, fast squatting, and fast squatting with elastic-band accommodating resistance in collegiate athletes.\n30:Do squat movement speed and accommodating resistance differentially affect jump power and squat strength?\n40:- Twelve-week randomized group intervention after three weeks of familiarization.\n41:- Groups used slow squats, fast squats, or fast squats plus elastic bands.\n46:- Jump power improved 17.8% in the fast-plus-bands group, 11.0% with fast squats, and 4.8% with slow squats. [p. 4]\n47:- Fast-plus-bands exceeded slow training for power improvement (p = .02); its difference from fast-only did not reach .05 (p = .09). [p. 5]\n48:- Squat-strength changes did not differ significantly among groups. [p. 5]\n49:- Fast-plus-bands and slow training had similar descriptive strength gains, about 9.4–9.6%. [p. 5]\n53:- Statistical power averaged only .67. [p. 4]\n56:- Band tension and bar-speed prescriptions require equipment-specific replication.\n60:Intent and velocity matter for power development, but the study does not prove bands are superior to every fast-load strategy. Use bands when they help preserve acceleration through the range and verify bar-speed and jump response.\n66:| Participants and grouping | p. 3 | method |\n67:| Power changes | p. 4 | result |\n68:| Between-group tests and strength | p. 5 | result and limitation |\n---\n11:doi: \"10.1080/14763140802266934\"\n22:> Jandacka, D., & Vaverka, F. (2008). A regression model to determine load for maximum power output. *Sports Biomechanics, 7*(3), 361–371. https://doi.org/10.1080/14763140802266934\n34:- Fifty-five male and 48 female physical-education students.\n48:- For the acceleration phase, estimated optimal bench-press loads were 56 ± 9% 1RM in men and 63 ± 8% 1RM in women. [p. 11]\n49:- Corresponding countermovement-squat loads were 47 ± 9% of maximum dynamic strength in men and 54 ± 9% in women. [p. 11]\n55:- The sample comprised young physical-education students rather than sport-specific elite athletes.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"de9d8a6a-a962-48a6-bdb3-ce034672f966","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
