"entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b3d56c70-7770-474c-abbb-ec68029cd07c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6ceed360-d6ad-4766-ba2d-4e6be6c75a36","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DuFaLVwTZqwzR5UqpEHrre","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.3\"\n     7\ttopic: accelerated eccentric and ballistic training\n     8\tcategory: [eccentric, plyometrics, power, overload]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Four Forms of Accelerated Powermetrics: Raising Force by Acceleration Instead of Added Mass\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The passage is written in the first person by one author (\"Let me now elaborate,\" PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415). Inference: it sits in the Siff and Simmons context of 7.3 (Fig 7.9 and \"The author has used ...\", PDF p. 422) and is probably Siff's, but the page does not name him. Bands can \"accelerate the load during the eccentric phase, thereby providing involuntary or passive acceleration,\" like plyometrics, where greater force in the amortisation phase comes from \"momentum acting on the body through the acceleration of gravity (e.g., during a drop jump) or the action of a propelled object.\" There is also \"an active or voluntary form ... which may be called actively accelerated powermetrics,\" usable in \"the bench press, squat, dip for the jerk, 'Romanian deadlift' (or mid range clean pull from the hang)\" and needing no partner or \"plyometric bench\" (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415, checked against the page image). The four forms (same page):\n    17\t\n    18\t1. Passively accelerated eccentrics with bands: \"allow the elastic tension to accelerate the bar during this phase. Then, after a suitable range of movement, halt the movement as rapidly as you can.\"\n    19\t2. Passively accelerated eccentrics with a partner, who pushes the bar down \"like an inverse form of \"forced reps\".\"\n    20\t3. Actively accelerated eccentrics: \"deliberately pull the bar downwards as fast as you can and stop the downward motion before you reach the end of the movement.\" Unlike bands, \"the force added to gravitational force ceases if one stops voluntarily accelerating the bar.\"\n    21\t4. Actively accelerated ballistics (rebounds): pull down as fast as possible, stop \"at a suitable point before the end of the movement and as rapidly as you can, try to accelerate the bar upwards into a powerful concentric movement.\"\n    22\t\n    23\t**Why it works** — The stated principle: \"this form of training, according to Newton's Second Law, focuses on force being increased by means of acceleration and not added mass\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416, checked against the page image). The author calls it \"really a type of 'supramaximal' force training\" (same page). No measurements of the forces produced are given.\n    24\t\n    25\t**When to apply** — When an athlete needs higher eccentric and amortisation forces than the bar weight alone supplies, without heavier loads or drop equipment. Start \"with relatively light loads in the bench press or squat (say, about 40-50kg, depending on your strength and training experience) and use very few repetitions,\" with \"considerably lower\" loads for smaller muscle groups (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). Load ceiling: \"maximal power production, depending on the specific exercise, occurs with loads of between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM, so it is unnecessary ever to exceed this range of loading in these accelerated training methods (unless one uses them over a very small range of motion)\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)). Inference: these are advanced options for that already-strong group, not for athletes still building relative strength.\n    26\t\n    27\t**When NOT to apply** — The author warns: \"do not overestimate your capabilities, because all of these forms of accelerated powermetrics can be very demanding on the body,\" and they \"can impose extremely large stresses on your soft tissues\" (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). For safety \"do not allow the accelerated load to force you into your extreme end position of joint action\" (PDF p. 426). No training study, injury data or force data are given: confidence `untested`. The book gives two power-load ranges six pages apart: \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411) and \"between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Both differ from the Chapter 3 figures of about 25% of maximal force in isotonic laboratory models and about two-thirds of maximum in the competition squat ([[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]]); Jandacka and Vaverka 2008 put the acceleration-phase optimum at 47-63% 1RM by sex and exercise in untrained students (vault brief, p. 11). Inference: treat 50-70% as a broad zone, not a fixed number.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Example** — The author's own starting point: an accelerated-eccentric bench press or squat with \"about 40-50kg\" and very few repetitions (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415). Inference: an actively accelerated ballistic bench at that load would be pulled down fast, stopped a few centimetres above the chest, and driven up at once.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (band-accelerated descent), [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]], [[research-papers/jandacka-2008-a-regression-model-to-determine-load-for-maximum-power-output]], [[research-papers/handford-2024-a-conceptual-framework-of-different-eccentric-training-methods]] (maps accelerated eccentric loading among eccentric methods), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.4.1, 7.4.2\"\n     7\ttopic: trunk muscle actions\n     8\tcategory: [trunk, functional-anatomy, rotation, exercise-selection]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Trunk Rotation Comes From the Obliques, Not the Erector Spinae, and Trunk Flexion Is Not Rectus Abdominis Alone\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Four trunk claims from 7.4.2 (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, checked against the page image): (1) the erector spinae \"Acting unilaterally ... serve to laterally extend the trunk, whereas acting bilaterally and symmetrically, they extend the trunk backwards without any lateral movement occurring\"; (2) \"lateral action of the long muscles of the back does not produce spinal rotation and ... the only muscle of the back which is involved in spinal rotation is multifidus. The major initiators of trunk rotation are the internal and external obliques and the strengthening of these muscles, therefore, is vital for all sporting activities that require rotation of the trunk\"; (3) rectus abdominis \"does not produce any lateral action of the trunk,\" while \"Both groups of obliques acting bilaterally produce trunk flexion so as to augment action by rectus abdominis, so it is incorrect to state that trunk flexion is solely a result of contraction of rectus abdominis\"; (4) \"if situps are done with a lateral flexing of the trunk, the rectus abdominis are not significantly involved, but the obliques and psoas are strongly contracted.\" The 7.4.1 summary gives rotation as \"The external abdominal obliques of one side acting in conjunction with the opposite internal oblique\" and lateral flexion as unilateral iliocostalis and longissimus, both obliques, quadratus lumborum and psoas (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417, checked against the page image).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the joint-action summary of 7.4.1 and the Movement Matrices of 3.7, which they say let us \"appreciate how ill understood some popular exercises are\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418). They give no EMG or cadaver data in this section.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — The authors' own scope is \"all sporting activities that require rotation of the trunk\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418). Inference: for throwers, batters and linemen who drive and turn, load rotation through the obliques rather than relying on back extensions, and when trunk flexion work is crunches only, add oblique-dominant patterns. Inference: the PNF trunk rotation pattern of Fig 7.8, done supine or standing with cable resistance, is the book's own tool for this (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns]]).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — The claim that multifidus is \"the only muscle of the back\" involved in spinal rotation is stated without citation. Inference: check this sentence against an anatomy reference before teaching it as exact. Claim (4) is given without data. Before any multi-joint analysis the authors themselves tell the reader to remember the end of Chapter 3: single-joint labels miss that \"muscles which do not span other joints can still produce acceleration about those joints\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416; [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]]). Confidence `supported` covers the broad joint-action claims, not the multifidus sentence.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own: sit-ups done with a lateral flex shift work from rectus abdominis to the obliques and psoas (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[framework--movement-matrix-system-for-multiarticular-exercise-selection]], [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]], [[heuristic--identify-moved-stabilized-and-both-joints-before-selecting-exercises]], [[claim--core-stability-balance-training-has-no-shown-transfer-to-sport]], [[framework--stretching-matrix-system-reverse-of-contraction-action]] (3.5, trunk actions used for stretching)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ad5faee9-9b29-4fe8-8107-a5e3e9d6a8e6","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:20:17.229Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: framework\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.3\"\n     7\ttopic: accelerated eccentric and ballistic training\n     8\tcategory: [eccentric, plyometrics, power, overload]\n     9\tconfidence: untested\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Four Forms of Accelerated Powermetrics: Raising Force by Acceleration Instead of Added Mass\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — The passage is written in the first person by one author (\"Let me now elaborate,\" PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415). Inference: it sits in the Siff and Simmons context of 7.3 (Fig 7.9 and \"The author has used ...\", PDF p. 422) and is probably Siff's, but the page does not name him. Bands can \"accelerate the load during the eccentric phase, thereby providing involuntary or passive acceleration,\" like plyometrics, where greater force in the amortisation phase comes from \"momentum acting on the body through the acceleration of gravity (e.g., during a drop jump) or the action of a propelled object.\" There is also \"an active or voluntary form ... which may be called actively accelerated powermetrics,\" usable in \"the bench press, squat, dip for the jerk, 'Romanian deadlift' (or mid range clean pull from the hang)\" and needing no partner or \"plyometric bench\" (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415, checked against the page image). The four forms (same page):\n    17\t\n    18\t1. Passively accelerated eccentrics with bands: \"allow the elastic tension to accelerate the bar during this phase. Then, after a suitable range of movement, halt the movement as rapidly as you can.\"\n    19\t2. Passively accelerated eccentrics with a partner, who pushes the bar down \"like an inverse form of \"forced reps\".\"\n    20\t3. Actively accelerated eccentrics: \"deliberately pull the bar downwards as fast as you can and stop the downward motion before you reach the end of the movement.\" Unlike bands, \"the force added to gravitational force ceases if one stops voluntarily accelerating the bar.\"\n    21\t4. Actively accelerated ballistics (rebounds): pull down as fast as possible, stop \"at a suitable point before the end of the movement and as rapidly as you can, try to accelerate the bar upwards into a powerful concentric movement.\"\n    22\t\n    23\t**Why it works** — The stated principle: \"this form of training, according to Newton's Second Law, focuses on force being increased by means of acceleration and not added mass\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416, checked against the page image). The author calls it \"really a type of 'supramaximal' force training\" (same page). No measurements of the forces produced are given.\n    24\t\n    25\t**When to apply** — When an athlete needs higher eccentric and amortisation forces than the bar weight alone supplies, without heavier loads or drop equipment. Start \"with relatively light loads in the bench press or squat (say, about 40-50kg, depending on your strength and training experience) and use very few repetitions,\" with \"considerably lower\" loads for smaller muscle groups (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). Load ceiling: \"maximal power production, depending on the specific exercise, occurs with loads of between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM, so it is unnecessary ever to exceed this range of loading in these accelerated training methods (unless one uses them over a very small range of motion)\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)). Inference: these are advanced options for that already-strong group, not for athletes still building relative strength.\n    26\t\n    27\t**When NOT to apply** — The author warns: \"do not overestimate your capabilities, because all of these forms of accelerated powermetrics can be very demanding on the body,\" and they \"can impose extremely large stresses on your soft tissues\" (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). For safety \"do not allow the accelerated load to force you into your extreme end position of joint action\" (PDF p. 426). No training study, injury data or force data are given: confidence `untested`. The book gives two power-load ranges six pages apart: \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411) and \"between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Both differ from the Chapter 3 figures of about 25% of maximal force in isotonic laboratory models and about two-thirds of maximum in the competition squat ([[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]]); Jandacka and Vaverka 2008 put the acceleration-phase optimum at 47-63% 1RM by sex and exercise in untrained students (vault brief, p. 11). Inference: treat 50-70% as a broad zone, not a fixed number.\n    28\t\n    29\t**Example** — The author's own starting point: an accelerated-eccentric bench press or squat with \"about 40-50kg\" and very few repetitions (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415). Inference: an actively accelerated ballistic bench at that load would be pulled down fast, stopped a few centimetres above the chest, and driven up at once.\n    30\t\n    31\t**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (band-accelerated descent), [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]], [[research-papers/jandacka-2008-a-regression-model-to-determine-load-for-maximum-power-output]], [[research-papers/handford-2024-a-conceptual-framework-of-different-eccentric-training-methods]] (maps accelerated eccentric loading among eccentric methods), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]\n     1\t---\n     2\ttype: library-entry\n     3\tkind: claim\n     4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n     5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n     6\tchapter: \"7.4.1, 7.4.2\"\n     7\ttopic: trunk muscle actions\n     8\tcategory: [trunk, functional-anatomy, rotation, exercise-selection]\n     9\tconfidence: supported\n    10\tingest-tier: full-text\n    11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n    12\t---\n    13\t\n    14\t# Trunk Rotation Comes From the Obliques, Not the Erector Spinae, and Trunk Flexion Is Not Rectus Abdominis Alone\n    15\t\n    16\t**What it is** — Four trunk claims from 7.4.2 (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, checked against the page image): (1) the erector spinae \"Acting unilaterally ... serve to laterally extend the trunk, whereas acting bilaterally and symmetrically, they extend the trunk backwards without any lateral movement occurring\"; (2) \"lateral action of the long muscles of the back does not produce spinal rotation and ... the only muscle of the back which is involved in spinal rotation is multifidus. The major initiators of trunk rotation are the internal and external obliques and the strengthening of these muscles, therefore, is vital for all sporting activities that require rotation of the trunk\"; (3) rectus abdominis \"does not produce any lateral action of the trunk,\" while \"Both groups of obliques acting bilaterally produce trunk flexion so as to augment action by rectus abdominis, so it is incorrect to state that trunk flexion is solely a result of contraction of rectus abdominis\"; (4) \"if situps are done with a lateral flexing of the trunk, the rectus abdominis are not significantly involved, but the obliques and psoas are strongly contracted.\" The 7.4.1 summary gives rotation as \"The external abdominal obliques of one side acting in conjunction with the opposite internal oblique\" and lateral flexion as unilateral iliocostalis and longissimus, both obliques, quadratus lumborum and psoas (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417, checked against the page image).\n    17\t\n    18\t**Why it works** — The authors' basis is the joint-action summary of 7.4.1 and the Movement Matrices of 3.7, which they say let us \"appreciate how ill understood some popular exercises are\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418). They give no EMG or cadaver data in this section.\n    19\t\n    20\t**When to apply** — The authors' own scope is \"all sporting activities that require rotation of the trunk\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418). Inference: for throwers, batters and linemen who drive and turn, load rotation through the obliques rather than relying on back extensions, and when trunk flexion work is crunches only, add oblique-dominant patterns. Inference: the PNF trunk rotation pattern of Fig 7.8, done supine or standing with cable resistance, is the book's own tool for this (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns]]).\n    21\t\n    22\t**When NOT to apply** — The claim that multifidus is \"the only muscle of the back\" involved in spinal rotation is stated without citation. Inference: check this sentence against an anatomy reference before teaching it as exact. Claim (4) is given without data. Before any multi-joint analysis the authors themselves tell the reader to remember the end of Chapter 3: single-joint labels miss that \"muscles which do not span other joints can still produce acceleration about those joints\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416; [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]]). Confidence `supported` covers the broad joint-action claims, not the multifidus sentence.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Example** — The authors' own: sit-ups done with a lateral flex shift work from rectus abdominis to the obliques and psoas (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418).\n    25\t\n    26\t**Related** — [[framework--movement-matrix-system-for-multiarticular-exercise-selection]], [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]], [[heuristic--identify-moved-stabilized-and-both-joints-before-selecting-exercises]], [[claim--core-stability-balance-training-has-no-shown-transfer-to-sport]], [[framework--stretching-matrix-system-reverse-of-contraction-action]] (3.5, trunk actions used for stretching)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b3d56c70-7770-474c-abbb-ec68029cd07c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
