{"parentUuid":"9adbf650-c90d-4fff-9286-8503db3d6180","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014iv2VRzp7LtPapukaqNeBc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: claim\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"7.2.3, 7.2.3.3\"\n7\ttopic: developmental progression in conditioning\n8\tcategory: [pnf, motor-learning, beginners, progression]\n9\tconfidence: untested\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Novices Should Progress From Mobility to Stability, Following the Developmental Sequences PNF Is Built On\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) writes that PNF methods \"were formulated from findings on neuromuscular development, such as the functional evolution of all movement from motor immaturity to motor maturity in the growing child or novice athlete,\" progressing \"total to individuated; proximal to distal, distal to proximal; mobile to stabile; gross to selective; reflexive to deliberate; overlapping to integrative; incoordinate to coordinate\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405, text layer). In 7.2.3.3 he restates the emphasis for \"the patient, the novice or the child\": \"progression from primitive to complex, gross to individuated, mobile to stabile, reflexive to deliberative, proximal to distal, and incoordinate to coordinate.\" Then: \"Contrary to common belief, the novice must be taught from a base of mobility to progress to stability, just as an infant learns to stand by first moving, staggering and exploring the environment\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — Siff's argument is the developmental analogy itself: the infant moves and staggers before standing stably (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). He also states that \"all physical conditioning requires a constant interplay of mobility and stability, all patterns implicate some parts of the body in stabilising and other parts in mobilising\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). He gives no training study for the novice-athlete claim.\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — When teaching a beginner a new pattern, let them explore the movement through range before demanding rigid holds, and progress gross to selective and primitive to complex. Principle 21, \"Planning of each phase to lay foundations for the next phase,\" and principle 20, \"Progression from primitive to complex actions\" (PDF p. 417 / printed p. 406), set the order. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver, not just a training-quality issue.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the PNF sequence is one way to order those gradual exercise-selection steps for a freshman entering a lifting program.\n21\t\n22\t**When NOT to apply** — This is an analogy from infant motor development extended to novice athletes, with no data in the book: confidence `untested`. The sequences are not one-directional. The 7.2.3 list gives both \"proximal to distal, distal to proximal\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405), the novice paragraph gives \"proximal to distal\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407), and principle 18 prescribes \"distal to proximal sequences in neuromuscularly mature subjects\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405). Inference: read these as direction by maturity (proximal to distal while immature, distal to proximal once mature), but the book does not say this in one place. Siff names the \"common belief\" he opposes but does not cite who holds it. For a trained athlete, the goal can be the opposite: \"the goal of training may be to prevent motion and to stimulate stability or maintain balance\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407).\n23\t\n24\t**Example** — Siff's example is developmental, not athletic: \"an infant learns to stand by first moving, staggering and exploring the environment\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). His athletic example of mixed demands: \"both static strength and static muscular endurance might have to be developed in the back muscles of an athlete, while three-dimensional dynamic strength, dynamic muscular endurance and dynamic flexibility might have to be developed in the upper leg\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407).\n25\t\n26\t**Related** — [[framework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system]], [[heuristic--identify-moved-stabilized-and-both-joints-before-selecting-exercises]] (moved and stabilised joints, 3.7), [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]]\n27\t"}]},"uuid":"4c99cdcf-2b8f-488a-9978-bcf33fc56bd8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:22:33.249Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.2.3, 7.2.3.3\"\ntopic: developmental progression in conditioning\ncategory: [pnf, motor-learning, beginners, progression]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Novices Should Progress From Mobility to Stability, Following the Developmental Sequences PNF Is Built On\n\n**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) writes that PNF methods \"were formulated from findings on neuromuscular development, such as the functional evolution of all movement from motor immaturity to motor maturity in the growing child or novice athlete,\" progressing \"total to individuated; proximal to distal, distal to proximal; mobile to stabile; gross to selective; reflexive to deliberate; overlapping to integrative; incoordinate to coordinate\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405, text layer). In 7.2.3.3 he restates the emphasis for \"the patient, the novice or the child\": \"progression from primitive to complex, gross to individuated, mobile to stabile, reflexive to deliberative, proximal to distal, and incoordinate to coordinate.\" Then: \"Contrary to common belief, the novice must be taught from a base of mobility to progress to stability, just as an infant learns to stand by first moving, staggering and exploring the environment\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer).\n\n**Why it works** — Siff's argument is the developmental analogy itself: the infant moves and staggers before standing stably (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). He also states that \"all physical conditioning requires a constant interplay of mobility and stability, all patterns implicate some parts of the body in stabilising and other parts in mobilising\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). He gives no training study for the novice-athlete claim.\n\n**When to apply** — When teaching a beginner a new pattern, let them explore the movement through range before demanding rigid holds, and progress gross to selective and primitive to complex. Principle 21, \"Planning of each phase to lay foundations for the next phase,\" and principle 20, \"Progression from primitive to complex actions\" (PDF p. 417 / printed p. 406), set the order. Eric's baseline: \"Progressions in exercise selection, volume, and intensity are kept smooth and gradual by design; abrupt, large changes are treated as a direct injury-risk driver, not just a training-quality issue.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 142, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the PNF sequence is one way to order those gradual exercise-selection steps for a freshman entering a lifting program.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — This is an analogy from infant motor development extended to novice athletes, with no data in the book: confidence `untested`. The sequences are not one-directional. The 7.2.3 list gives both \"proximal to distal, distal to proximal\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405), the novice paragraph gives \"proximal to distal\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407), and principle 18 prescribes \"distal to proximal sequences in neuromuscularly mature subjects\" (PDF p. 416 / printed p. 405). Inference: read these as direction by maturity (proximal to distal while immature, distal to proximal once mature), but the book does not say this in one place. Siff names the \"common belief\" he opposes but does not cite who holds it. For a trained athlete, the goal can be the opposite: \"the goal of training may be to prevent motion and to stimulate stability or maintain balance\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407).\n\n**Example** — Siff's example is developmental, not athletic: \"an infant learns to stand by first moving, staggering and exploring the environment\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). His athletic example of mixed demands: \"both static strength and static muscular endurance might have to be developed in the back muscles of an athlete, while three-dimensional dynamic strength, dynamic muscular endurance and dynamic flexibility might have to be developed in the upper leg\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system]], [[heuristic--identify-moved-stabilized-and-both-joints-before-selecting-exercises]] (moved and stabilised joints, 3.7), [[claim--muscle-isolation-is-load-dependent-mostly-illusory-under-heavy-load]]\n","numLines":27,"startLine":1,"totalLines":27}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9adbf650-c90d-4fff-9286-8503db3d6180","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4c99cdcf-2b8f-488a-9978-bcf33fc56bd8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffr1fqaLbgri6pzSHQyF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014LBgM4Kb4jZxNtTHYhZias","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1717,"cache_read_input_tokens":156893,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1717,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014LBgM4Kb4jZxNtTHYhZias":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":7,"requestId":"req_011Cffr1V7A2c3i84coMKs94","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"c25a0ee1-faca-40f5-950f-da639b6862e4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:22:34.053Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c25a0ee1-faca-40f5-950f-da639b6862e4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014LBgM4Kb4jZxNtTHYhZias","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: heuristic\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"7.2.3.3, 7.2.4, 7.5\"\n7\ttopic: equipment for PNF patterns\n8\tcategory: [pnf, equipment, exercise-selection, machines]\n9\tconfidence: untested\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Rank Equipment by How Well It Allows Spiral-Diagonal Patterns: Pulleys and Dumbbells First, Barbells Next, Machines Last\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) ranks equipment for modified PNF: \"Pulleys and dumbbells are especially suitable for application of the spiral and diagonal, midline-crossing PNF techniques. ... Barbell training provides fewer opportunities for the use of PNF training, while machines offer the fewest possibilities of all\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410, checked against the page image). Patterns are \"Probably the most neglected aspect of PNF by coaches\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer). \"No machines other than the traditional high-low pulley machines allow the user to produce PNF patterns,\" and \"pec-decks, back hyperextension machines, seated pressing machines, hack-squat machines and leg extension machines ... cause such large deviations from recommended PNF patterns that they can impose excessive loading on certain joints and are suitable only for a small proportion of the training of highly conditioned athletes\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer). Machine training \"requires a disproportionate number of machines to approximate the training offered by free weight and pulley training in three-dimensional space\" (same page).\n17\t\n18\t**Why it works** — Siff's reason is pattern fidelity: PNF patterns \"stimulate more thorough involvement and motor development than the less-than-optimal patterns imposed by the abundance of linear or cam machines\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). Machine design \"imposes body positions, postures and activation of pivots which usually differ considerably from those recommended by PNF and compromise the effectiveness and safety of the exercise\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410). He calls the belief that machines offer \"greater stability and safety for beginners than free weights\" \"A major error,\" and concludes \"instructors must always 'spot' for the users of machines as well as free weights\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410).\n19\t\n20\t**When to apply** — When choosing assistance exercises for rotational or overhead athletes, prefer cable and dumbbell versions that let the limb cross the midline and rotate. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: this heuristic applies inside that small assistance share; it does not argue for replacing the barbell core lifts.\n21\t\n22\t**When NOT to apply** — Siff says it \"does not imply that barbell and machine training should be avoided\": they \"can be used effectively under special circumstances, sometimes to cause intentional deviations from PNF patterns to adapt the body to potentially harmful actions which may occur in real sporting situations\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410). No comparative data are given for the ranking or for the joint-loading claims about named machines. The \"excessive loading\" judgment on pec-decks and leg extensions is Siff's opinion in this passage. Inference: in rehab settings where Athletic Medicine wants a fixed path, a machine's constraint can be the point.\n23\t\n24\t**Example** — Siff's examples: \"The standing full-range cable cross-over exercise with external-internal rotation of the shoulder joint furnishes just one example of an adapted PNF pattern\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410); and in 7.5, for a baseball pitcher or javelin thrower, cable-crossovers \"in a specific spiral-diagonal pattern ... that cross the sagittal midline of the body\" instead of the pec-deck (PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419, checked against the page image). Fig 7.8's caption adds that the trunk rotation pattern can be done supine or standing and \"Resistance may be offered with a cable machine\" (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image).\n25\t\n26\t**Related** — [[framework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system]], [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (4.2.7.1), [[heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use]] (4.2.7, the same \"not safer than free weights\" point), [[heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines]], [[principle--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces]]\n27\t"}]},"uuid":"dcbf758c-e29b-419b-86b8-d7863d4c5d1a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:22:34.226Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.2.3.3, 7.2.4, 7.5\"\ntopic: equipment for PNF patterns\ncategory: [pnf, equipment, exercise-selection, machines]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Rank Equipment by How Well It Allows Spiral-Diagonal Patterns: Pulleys and Dumbbells First, Barbells Next, Machines Last\n\n**What it is** — Siff (7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419) ranks equipment for modified PNF: \"Pulleys and dumbbells are especially suitable for application of the spiral and diagonal, midline-crossing PNF techniques. ... Barbell training provides fewer opportunities for the use of PNF training, while machines offer the fewest possibilities of all\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410, checked against the page image). Patterns are \"Probably the most neglected aspect of PNF by coaches\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer). \"No machines other than the traditional high-low pulley machines allow the user to produce PNF patterns,\" and \"pec-decks, back hyperextension machines, seated pressing machines, hack-squat machines and leg extension machines ... cause such large deviations from recommended PNF patterns that they can impose excessive loading on certain joints and are suitable only for a small proportion of the training of highly conditioned athletes\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407, text layer). Machine training \"requires a disproportionate number of machines to approximate the training offered by free weight and pulley training in three-dimensional space\" (same page).\n\n**Why it works** — Siff's reason is pattern fidelity: PNF patterns \"stimulate more thorough involvement and motor development than the less-than-optimal patterns imposed by the abundance of linear or cam machines\" (PDF p. 418 / printed p. 407). Machine design \"imposes body positions, postures and activation of pivots which usually differ considerably from those recommended by PNF and compromise the effectiveness and safety of the exercise\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410). He calls the belief that machines offer \"greater stability and safety for beginners than free weights\" \"A major error,\" and concludes \"instructors must always 'spot' for the users of machines as well as free weights\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410).\n\n**When to apply** — When choosing assistance exercises for rotational or overhead athletes, prefer cable and dumbbell versions that let the limb cross the midline and rotate. Eric's baseline: \"Training time is deliberately weighted toward high motor-unit-recruitment core/ballistic movements (squats, cleans, jumps, throws, sprints); single-joint assistance work is kept to a small share of total volume.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 104, Inferred (transcript + slides)). Inference: this heuristic applies inside that small assistance share; it does not argue for replacing the barbell core lifts.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Siff says it \"does not imply that barbell and machine training should be avoided\": they \"can be used effectively under special circumstances, sometimes to cause intentional deviations from PNF patterns to adapt the body to potentially harmful actions which may occur in real sporting situations\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410). No comparative data are given for the ranking or for the joint-loading claims about named machines. The \"excessive loading\" judgment on pec-decks and leg extensions is Siff's opinion in this passage. Inference: in rehab settings where Athletic Medicine wants a fixed path, a machine's constraint can be the point.\n\n**Example** — Siff's examples: \"The standing full-range cable cross-over exercise with external-internal rotation of the shoulder joint furnishes just one example of an adapted PNF pattern\" (PDF p. 421 / printed p. 410); and in 7.5, for a baseball pitcher or javelin thrower, cable-crossovers \"in a specific spiral-diagonal pattern ... that cross the sagittal midline of the body\" instead of the pec-deck (PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419, checked against the page image). Fig 7.8's caption adds that the trunk rotation pattern can be done supine or standing and \"Resistance may be offered with a cable machine\" (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system]], [[framework--functional-vs-nonfunctional-resistance-machines]] (4.2.7.1), [[heuristic--nfr-machine-limitations-safety-and-appropriate-use]] (4.2.7, the same \"not safer than free weights\" point), [[heuristic--equip-circuits-with-free-weights-and-pulleys-over-multistation-machines]], [[principle--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces]]\n","numLines":27,"startLine":1,"totalLines":27}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c25a0ee1-faca-40f5-950f-da639b6862e4","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
