{"parentUuid":"eedb24a8-111f-48bf-affa-ef8154147fc3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrE5M2tfokzuK7xHG2H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014xGNs5euCPrp6dkfVJsmsx","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","old_string":"confidence: untested","new_string":"confidence: supported"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":550,"cache_read_input_tokens":171933,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":550,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_014xGNs5euCPrp6dkfVJsmsx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","old_string":"confidence: untested","new_string":"confidence: supported"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffrDtVAuTE4vepcMCjno","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"859ce17f-20a5-4041-8916-296322c5e430","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:33.546Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"859ce17f-20a5-4041-8916-296322c5e430","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014xGNs5euCPrp6dkfVJsmsx","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"8d16c21e-5f84-40a2-93c5-7d8f2e533911","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:33.658Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","oldString":"confidence: untested","newString":"confidence: supported","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.3\"\ntopic: combined free weight and isokinetic resistance\ncategory: [variable-resistance, isokinetic, power, biomechanics]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Fradd (1995): Elbow Flexion Under Free Weight, Isokinetic and Combined Resistance\n\n**What it is** — The authors report a study in which \"the same group of subjects\" did elbow flexion under free-weight (isoinertial), isokinetic, and combined resistance, the combination applied \"using a cable machine specially designed and constructed for this purpose by one of Siff's mechanical engineering students at the University of the Witwatersrand in South Africa (Fradd, 1995)\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image). Number of subjects, their training status, loads and isokinetic speed are not given. Table 7.1 (velocity in m/s, power in W, force in N):\n\n| Resistance | Max velocity | Mean power | Peak power | Mean force | Peak force |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Isoinertial | 1.49 | 151 | 306 | 184 | 219 |\n| Isokinetic | 0.42 | 80 | 129 | 247 | 350 |\n| Combined | 0.38 | 102 | 131 | 336 | 380 |\n\nThe five stated findings: (1) \"Greatest mean and peak power is produced under free weight conditions, reaching a peak approximately midway through the movement\"; (2) \"Free weight force production is greatest during the earliest phases of joint movement\"; (3) isokinetic and combined power is \"considerably lower\" but \"remains near its peak value for a considerable part of the range\"; (4) \"Mean and peak force production is greatest during combined conditions, and may peak twice during the movement, most commonly near the beginning and the end of range\"; (5) \"Isokinetic force plateaus about one-third through the range, but commonly increases near end of range\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). For Fig 7.10 the text adds that peak power and velocity come \"about halfway through the movement, when elbow angle is approximately 90 degrees,\" that \"Approximately half the movement is spent in acceleration and half in deceleration,\" and that free-weight peak power is \"some three times greater than under isokinetic or combined auxotonic-isokinetic conditions\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the differences beyond the resistance types themselves. They stress variability: curve shape \"varies with every individual, according to their motor characteristics, and, in the combined scenario, with the magnitude of the inertial load and the velocity of isokinetic resistance\"; combined resistance \"may be made more auxotonic (free weight) if the added weight is large and more isokinetic if the added load is relatively small\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). Inference: free weight lets the load accelerate and then forces deceleration, which yields high peak power mid-range; an isokinetic device caps velocity, so force can stay high while power stays low.\n\n**When to apply** — When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424). (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s.\n\n**Example** — The study itself: in the combined condition, mean force was 336 N against 184 N under free weight, while peak power was 131 W against 306 W (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (the second hybrid in 7.3), [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] (4.2.6.1), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (the authors' free-weight alternative, named in the 7.3 opening), [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]] (mechanics of constant, isokinetic and variable resistance)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":6,"oldLines":7,"newStart":6,"newLines":7,"lines":[" chapter: \"7.3\""," topic: combined free weight and isokinetic resistance"," category: [variable-resistance, isokinetic, power, biomechanics]","-confidence: untested","+confidence: supported"," ingest-tier: full-text"," created: 2026-10-03"," ---"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"859ce17f-20a5-4041-8916-296322c5e430","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8d16c21e-5f84-40a2-93c5-7d8f2e533911","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrE5M2tfokzuK7xHG2H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0179VxiuGQ42RjHFfzrxCuzo","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","old_string":"One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424).","new_string":"One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.3-2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 345 against about 115-125 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412) starts at about 165-170 and peaks at about 225-230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413) rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, and is highest at about 370-375 near 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end: the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":550,"cache_read_input_tokens":171933,"output_tokens":1250,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":550},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1250,"cache_read_input_tokens":171933,"cache_creation_input_tokens":550,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":550,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0179VxiuGQ42RjHFfzrxCuzo":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","old_string":"One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424).","new_string":"One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.3-2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 345 against about 115-125 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412) starts at about 165-170 and peaks at about 225-230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413) rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, and is highest at about 370-375 near 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end: the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffrDtVAuTE4vepcMCjno","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2ffa2f7d-b204-4019-a06a-751b3efecf24","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:40.682Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2ffa2f7d-b204-4019-a06a-751b3efecf24","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0179VxiuGQ42RjHFfzrxCuzo","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"591a103e-f76e-48b6-acea-5c1a71f8b395","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:40.793Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","oldString":"One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424).","newString":"One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.3-2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 345 against about 115-125 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412) starts at about 165-170 and peaks at about 225-230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413) rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, and is highest at about 370-375 near 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end: the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.3\"\ntopic: combined free weight and isokinetic resistance\ncategory: [variable-resistance, isokinetic, power, biomechanics]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Fradd (1995): Elbow Flexion Under Free Weight, Isokinetic and Combined Resistance\n\n**What it is** — The authors report a study in which \"the same group of subjects\" did elbow flexion under free-weight (isoinertial), isokinetic, and combined resistance, the combination applied \"using a cable machine specially designed and constructed for this purpose by one of Siff's mechanical engineering students at the University of the Witwatersrand in South Africa (Fradd, 1995)\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image). Number of subjects, their training status, loads and isokinetic speed are not given. Table 7.1 (velocity in m/s, power in W, force in N):\n\n| Resistance | Max velocity | Mean power | Peak power | Mean force | Peak force |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Isoinertial | 1.49 | 151 | 306 | 184 | 219 |\n| Isokinetic | 0.42 | 80 | 129 | 247 | 350 |\n| Combined | 0.38 | 102 | 131 | 336 | 380 |\n\nThe five stated findings: (1) \"Greatest mean and peak power is produced under free weight conditions, reaching a peak approximately midway through the movement\"; (2) \"Free weight force production is greatest during the earliest phases of joint movement\"; (3) isokinetic and combined power is \"considerably lower\" but \"remains near its peak value for a considerable part of the range\"; (4) \"Mean and peak force production is greatest during combined conditions, and may peak twice during the movement, most commonly near the beginning and the end of range\"; (5) \"Isokinetic force plateaus about one-third through the range, but commonly increases near end of range\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). For Fig 7.10 the text adds that peak power and velocity come \"about halfway through the movement, when elbow angle is approximately 90 degrees,\" that \"Approximately half the movement is spent in acceleration and half in deceleration,\" and that free-weight peak power is \"some three times greater than under isokinetic or combined auxotonic-isokinetic conditions\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413, checked against the page image).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for the differences beyond the resistance types themselves. They stress variability: curve shape \"varies with every individual, according to their motor characteristics, and, in the combined scenario, with the magnitude of the inertial load and the velocity of isokinetic resistance\"; combined resistance \"may be made more auxotonic (free weight) if the added weight is large and more isokinetic if the added load is relatively small\" (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412). Inference: free weight lets the load accelerate and then forces deceleration, which yields high peak power mid-range; an isokinetic device caps velocity, so force can stay high while power stays low.\n\n**When to apply** — When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424). (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s.\n\n**Example** — The study itself: in the combined condition, mean force was 336 N against 184 N under free weight, while peak power was 131 W against 306 W (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (the second hybrid in 7.3), [[framework--isokinetic-method-claimed-advantages-and-mechanism]] (4.2.6.1), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]] (the authors' free-weight alternative, named in the 7.3 opening), [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]] (mechanics of constant, isokinetic and variable resistance)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":27,"oldLines":7,"newStart":27,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides))."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, one unreported sample, a student project: confidence `untested`. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF pp. 423-424). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 350 against about 125-130 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve starts at about 170 and peaks at about 230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve rises to about 350 by about 30%, holds, and is highest at about 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end; the double peak \"near the beginning and the end of range\" in finding 4 is not visible in this printed curve (PDF p. 424). (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s.","+**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.3-2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 345 against about 115-125 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412) starts at about 165-170 and peaks at about 225-230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413) rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, and is highest at about 370-375 near 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end: the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows. (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s."," "," **Example** — The study itself: in the combined condition, mean force was 336 N against 184 N under free weight, while peak power was 131 W against 306 W (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2ffa2f7d-b204-4019-a06a-751b3efecf24","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
