{"parentUuid":"96fb127e-f245-4294-b604-39f0b4d3bbc5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrFC9LcQzrU474gMcuU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DZ2YrwZoeeTctXRCrFQWVA","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","old_string":"Item 4 follows from the band tension rising as the lifter stands.","new_string":"Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising as the lifter stands; the book does not state the mechanism."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1508,"cache_read_input_tokens":172483,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1508,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DZ2YrwZoeeTctXRCrFQWVA":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","old_string":"Item 4 follows from the band tension rising as the lifter stands.","new_string":"Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising as the lifter stands; the book does not state the mechanism."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffrEn4EwsYU3e5fmsLEa","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e2ead5ba-2b4c-4d5e-a3f6-a7c332dc0136","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:51.119Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"e2ead5ba-2b4c-4d5e-a3f6-a7c332dc0136","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DZ2YrwZoeeTctXRCrFQWVA","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"ea917156-db95-43a0-8d34-4a6d184f087d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:51.222Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","oldString":"Item 4 follows from the band tension rising as the lifter stands.","newString":"Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising as the lifter stands; the book does not state the mechanism.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.3\"\ntopic: bands and chains with free weights\ncategory: [variable-resistance, accommodating-resistance, eccentric, powerlifting]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Box Squat With Free Weights Plus Elastic Bands: Greater Force Through the Range and a Faster Descent\n\n**What it is** — The authors describe a box squat in a power rack \"with strong elastic bands attached between the ends of the bar and the base of the rack,\" band length set so resistance \"varied between set limits at the lowest and the highest positions\" (PDF pp. 424-425 / printed pp. 413-414, checked against the page images). The powerlifters (number not given) had \"in addition to the squat weight of 200kg, an added elastic resistance of 150kg at the lowest position while sitting down on the box with knees at approximately right angles and a greatest elastic resistance of 250kg at the top of the squat with knees fully extended\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The stated differences from free-weight training: \"1. A greater mean and peak force were produced throughout the range of movement. 2. The descent onto the box tended to be accelerated above the normal gravitational rate of 9.8m/sec squared, so that greater eccentric force had to be generated to control the downward motion. 3. The stronger eccentric loading and the brief transition period involved while sitting before exploding upwards provided neuromuscular stimulation which approximates that usually encountered in popular plyometric training. 4. The force generated during the later stages increased, in strong contrast to the situation of normal squatting in which force production tends to decrease significantly\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Fig 7.13 (checked against the page image) plots force as a percentage of the bar weight against time: the elastic component runs from about 125% at the top to 75% at the bottom (150/200 and 250/200, matching the text); combined resistance runs from about 225% at the top to about 175% at the bottom and back to about 225%; the eccentric phase spans about 0 to 2.3 s, the box transition about 2.3 to 3.1 s, and the concentric phase about 3.1 to 6 s. The force attributed to the weight itself dips to about 90% during the descent and rises to about 120% early in the ascent.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is items 2 and 3 above: band recoil accelerates the descent beyond gravity, demanding more eccentric force, and the short pause on the box before driving up resembles plyometric loading (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Item 4 follows from the band tension rising as the lifter stands. The 7.3 opening gives the general reason for variable resistance: force capacity \"changes with joint angle,\" so \"any given load will tend to underload certain muscles over some part of the movement range\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image).\n\n**When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5). The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: 200 kg on the bar, bands adding 150 kg on the box and 250 kg at lockout, so the lifter meets about 350 kg at the bottom and about 450 kg at the top (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414; the totals are this entry's arithmetic from the stated values, and match Fig 7.13's 175% and 225%).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion]], [[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]], [[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]], [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]], [[research-papers/rhea-2009-alterations-in-speed-of-squat-movement-and-the-use-of-accommodated-resistance-among-colleg]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — The authors describe a box squat in a power rack \"with strong elastic bands attached between the ends of the bar and the base of the rack,\" band length set so resistance \"varied between set limits at the lowest and the highest positions\" (PDF pp. 424-425 / printed pp. 413-414, checked against the page images). The powerlifters (number not given) had \"in addition to the squat weight of 200kg, an added elastic resistance of 150kg at the lowest position while sitting down on the box with knees at approximately right angles and a greatest elastic resistance of 250kg at the top of the squat with knees fully extended\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The stated differences from free-weight training: \"1. A greater mean and peak force were produced throughout the range of movement. 2. The descent onto the box tended to be accelerated above the normal gravitational rate of 9.8m/sec squared, so that greater eccentric force had to be generated to control the downward motion. 3. The stronger eccentric loading and the brief transition period involved while sitting before exploding upwards provided neuromuscular stimulation which approximates that usually encountered in popular plyometric training. 4. The force generated during the later stages increased, in strong contrast to the situation of normal squatting in which force production tends to decrease significantly\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Fig 7.13 (checked against the page image) plots force as a percentage of the bar weight against time: the elastic component runs from about 125% at the top to 75% at the bottom (150/200 and 250/200, matching the text); combined resistance runs from about 225% at the top to about 175% at the bottom and back to about 225%; the eccentric phase spans about 0 to 2.3 s, the box transition about 2.3 to 3.1 s, and the concentric phase about 3.1 to 6 s. The force attributed to the weight itself dips to about 90% during the descent and rises to about 120% early in the ascent."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is items 2 and 3 above: band recoil accelerates the descent beyond gravity, demanding more eccentric force, and the short pause on the box before driving up resembles plyometric loading (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Item 4 follows from the band tension rising as the lifter stands. The 7.3 opening gives the general reason for variable resistance: force capacity \"changes with joint angle,\" so \"any given load will tend to underload certain muscles over some part of the movement range\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image).","+**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is items 2 and 3 above: band recoil accelerates the descent beyond gravity, demanding more eccentric force, and the short pause on the box before driving up resembles plyometric loading (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising as the lifter stands; the book does not state the mechanism. The 7.3 opening gives the general reason for variable resistance: force capacity \"changes with joint angle,\" so \"any given load will tend to underload certain muscles over some part of the movement range\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image)."," "," **When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e2ead5ba-2b4c-4d5e-a3f6-a7c332dc0136","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ea917156-db95-43a0-8d34-4a6d184f087d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrFC9LcQzrU474gMcuU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NTVjdU8XdhSt4xAQgVDnWh","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","old_string":"That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5).","new_string":"Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Frost's acute summary (bands \"may sustain ... late-phase force\") does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1508,"cache_read_input_tokens":172483,"output_tokens":1152,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":206},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1508},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1152,"cache_read_input_tokens":172483,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1508,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1508,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01NTVjdU8XdhSt4xAQgVDnWh":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","old_string":"That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5).","new_string":"Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Frost's acute summary (bands \"may sustain ... late-phase force\") does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffrEn4EwsYU3e5fmsLEa","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4009471e-1af2-43e6-b6c7-46a1533feb1d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:56.545Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4009471e-1af2-43e6-b6c7-46a1533feb1d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NTVjdU8XdhSt4xAQgVDnWh","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md has been updated successfully."}]},"uuid":"df3967ce-3f35-449d-8f5b-7d7072c74515","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:23:56.663Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","oldString":"That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5).","newString":"Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Frost's acute summary (bands \"may sustain ... late-phase force\") does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: case-study\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"7.3\"\ntopic: bands and chains with free weights\ncategory: [variable-resistance, accommodating-resistance, eccentric, powerlifting]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Box Squat With Free Weights Plus Elastic Bands: Greater Force Through the Range and a Faster Descent\n\n**What it is** — The authors describe a box squat in a power rack \"with strong elastic bands attached between the ends of the bar and the base of the rack,\" band length set so resistance \"varied between set limits at the lowest and the highest positions\" (PDF pp. 424-425 / printed pp. 413-414, checked against the page images). The powerlifters (number not given) had \"in addition to the squat weight of 200kg, an added elastic resistance of 150kg at the lowest position while sitting down on the box with knees at approximately right angles and a greatest elastic resistance of 250kg at the top of the squat with knees fully extended\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The stated differences from free-weight training: \"1. A greater mean and peak force were produced throughout the range of movement. 2. The descent onto the box tended to be accelerated above the normal gravitational rate of 9.8m/sec squared, so that greater eccentric force had to be generated to control the downward motion. 3. The stronger eccentric loading and the brief transition period involved while sitting before exploding upwards provided neuromuscular stimulation which approximates that usually encountered in popular plyometric training. 4. The force generated during the later stages increased, in strong contrast to the situation of normal squatting in which force production tends to decrease significantly\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Fig 7.13 (checked against the page image) plots force as a percentage of the bar weight against time: the elastic component runs from about 125% at the top to 75% at the bottom (150/200 and 250/200, matching the text); combined resistance runs from about 225% at the top to about 175% at the bottom and back to about 225%; the eccentric phase spans about 0 to 2.3 s, the box transition about 2.3 to 3.1 s, and the concentric phase about 3.1 to 6 s. The force attributed to the weight itself dips to about 90% during the descent and rises to about 120% early in the ascent.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' explanation is items 2 and 3 above: band recoil accelerates the descent beyond gravity, demanding more eccentric force, and the short pause on the box before driving up resembles plyometric loading (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising as the lifter stands; the book does not state the mechanism. The 7.3 opening gives the general reason for variable resistance: force capacity \"changes with joint angle,\" so \"any given load will tend to underload certain muscles over some part of the movement range\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image).\n\n**When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5). The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: 200 kg on the bar, bands adding 150 kg on the box and 250 kg at lockout, so the lifter meets about 350 kg at the bottom and about 450 kg at the top (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414; the totals are this entry's arithmetic from the stated values, and match Fig 7.13's 175% and 225%).\n\n**Related** — [[case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion]], [[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]], [[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]], [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]], [[research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance]], [[research-papers/rhea-2009-alterations-in-speed-of-squat-movement-and-the-use-of-accommodated-resistance-among-colleg]], [[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). That is a named library source against the authors' item 1 and their claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). In the other direction, Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) found jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5). The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar.","+**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Frost's acute summary (bands \"may sustain ... late-phase force\") does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences. The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: 200 kg on the bar, bands adding 150 kg on the box and 250 kg at lockout, so the lifter meets about 350 kg at the bottom and about 450 kg at the top (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414; the totals are this entry's arithmetic from the stated values, and match Fig 7.13's 175% and 225%)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false,"contentNotInModelContext":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4009471e-1af2-43e6-b6c7-46a1533feb1d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
