{"parentUuid":"42a3f796-9b1c-4cdd-b78b-54c9c0ff4158","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrG713WRU7tU37qSZE3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KuLRVDc7DRP1WtAPt2XRCx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && perl -pi -e 's/\\QFrost'\"'\"'s acute summary (bands \"may sustain ... late-phase force\") does not\\E/Frost'\"'\"'s acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21) does not/' case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md && grep -o \"Frost's acute summary[^)]*)\" case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1398,"cache_read_input_tokens":173991,"output_tokens":467,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":207},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1398},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":467,"cache_read_input_tokens":173991,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1398,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1398,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01KuLRVDc7DRP1WtAPt2XRCx":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && perl -pi -e 's/\\QFrost'\"'\"'s acute summary (bands \"may sustain ... late-phase force\") does not\\E/Frost'\"'\"'s acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21) does not/' case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md && grep -o \"Frost's acute summary[^)]*)\" case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffrFwYHUcb2ZzdE4EQZ5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4a7a8955-566c-4747-9a9a-cfd3bf7c9bdd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:24:03.625Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4a7a8955-566c-4747-9a9a-cfd3bf7c9bdd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KuLRVDc7DRP1WtAPt2XRCx","type":"tool_result","content":"Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ee67a15e-0ebb-4dcf-aae9-21edd64a4e13","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:24:04.049Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — To raise force late in the range of a squat, deadlift, bench or clean where the free-weight load underloads the stronger positions. The authors extend the method to \"the deadlift, the clean, the bench press, good mornings and one arm deadlifts,\" to bands between fixed and moving parts of pulley machines, and to chains, \"pioneered in the USA by Louie Simmons primarily for powerlifting purposes,\" where \"as the weight is lifted further off the floor, the more links of chain are raised and the more the resistance increases\" (PDF pp. 425-426 / printed pp. 414-415, checked against the page images). Calibration: stand on a bathroom scale holding the bar with stretched cable at the start and end positions, or hang loads from a band to draw \"a load vs extension graph\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). For power, the authors cap loads at \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411). Eric's baseline: \"Prilepin's chart (total reps and reps-per-set by %1RM) is the default guide for daily rep/set assignment within a training block\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 107, Inferred/Explicit (shared team material)). Inference: with bands, the bar's %1RM no longer describes the load at the top or bottom, so a Prilepin prescription needs the band tension stated beside it."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Frost's acute summary (bands \"may sustain ... late-phase force\") does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences. The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar.","+**When NOT to apply** — Contested. Frost et al. 2010 (a narrative biomechanical review) state that bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, but \"improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights\" (vault brief, review p. 21). Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity, vault brief, p. 21) does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea et al. 2009 (48 NCAA Division I men, 12 weeks) bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not on items 1-4, which are acute force measures: jump power rose 17.8% with fast squats plus bands, 11.0% with fast squats and 4.8% with slow squats; bands beat slow training (p = .02) but not fast-only training (p = .09), and squat strength did not differ (vault brief, pp. 4-5); the brief also gives statistical power of only .67 (p. 4). Inference: Rhea gives partial support for bands in power training but does not separate the bands from the fast bar speed, so `contested` here is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim, not to the measured force differences. The authors themselves warn of \"large individual variations\" and that the curve \"depends on the magnitude of the weights load relative to the top and bottom resistance provided by the elastic bands\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414). The study is described without sample size or statistics. A faster, band-driven descent raises eccentric force, so the authors' caution on accelerated eccentrics applies ([[framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics]]). Inference: this is not in conflict with the 4.2.2 warning that pure elastic resistance \"begins from zero\" and should not be used for starting or explosive strength ([[framework--inertial-flywheel-and-elastic-band-resistance-devices]]), because here the bands sit on top of a 200 kg bar."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: 200 kg on the bar, bands adding 150 kg on the box and 250 kg at lockout, so the lifter meets about 350 kg at the bottom and about 450 kg at the top (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414; the totals are this entry's arithmetic from the stated values, and match Fig 7.13's 175% and 225%)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4a7a8955-566c-4747-9a9a-cfd3bf7c9bdd","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
