rash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && f=framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md && perl -pi -e 's/\\Qno force data for the comparison ([[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]])\\E/no force data for the comparison ([[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]], 4.2.3)/; s/\\Q**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (band-accelerated descent), \\E/**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (band-accelerated descent), [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]] (the 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods), /' $f && grep -o \"kinetic-energy-stimulation[^]]*\\]\\][^,.]*\" $f"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffrJFFT3uShvBp4Rfe3Z","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d673ed2b-f7d1-4e3c-b987-89a0ed2ee173","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:24:34.494Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d673ed2b-f7d1-4e3c-b987-89a0ed2ee173","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FfZhFU3BYpBxTrdSnrusmv","type":"tool_result","content":"kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]\nkinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]] (the 4","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"619b67e5-8c2b-4ced-baa0-6d4ffaa06ac4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:24:34.788Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]]\nkinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]] (the 4","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":24,"oldLines":8,"newStart":24,"newLines":8,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — When an athlete needs higher eccentric and amortisation forces than the bar weight alone supplies, without heavier loads or drop equipment. Start \"with relatively light loads in the bench press or squat (say, about 40-50kg, depending on your strength and training experience) and use very few repetitions,\" with \"considerably lower\" loads for smaller muscle groups (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). Load ceiling: \"maximal power production, depending on the specific exercise, occurs with loads of between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM, so it is unnecessary ever to exceed this range of loading in these accelerated training methods (unless one uses them over a very small range of motion)\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)). Inference: these are advanced options for that already-strong group, not for athletes still building relative strength."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The author warns: \"do not overestimate your capabilities, because all of these forms of accelerated powermetrics can be very demanding on the body,\" and they \"can impose extremely large stresses on your soft tissues\" (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). For safety \"do not allow the accelerated load to force you into your extreme end position of joint action\" (PDF p. 426). No training study, injury data or force data are given: confidence `untested`. Inference: the \"even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods\" comparison (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415) puts these forms at or above the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods in intensity, so they belong after those methods in a progression, but the book gives no force data for the comparison ([[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]]). The book gives two power-load ranges six pages apart: \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411) and \"between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Both differ from the Chapter 3 figures of about 25% of maximal force in isotonic laboratory models and about two-thirds of maximum in the competition squat ([[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]]); Jandacka and Vaverka 2008 put the acceleration-phase optimum at 47-63% 1RM by sex and exercise in physical-education students (55 men, 48 women) (vault brief, p. 11). Inference: treat 50-70% as a broad zone, not a fixed number.","+**When NOT to apply** — The author warns: \"do not overestimate your capabilities, because all of these forms of accelerated powermetrics can be very demanding on the body,\" and they \"can impose extremely large stresses on your soft tissues\" (PDF pp. 426-427 / printed pp. 415-416). For safety \"do not allow the accelerated load to force you into your extreme end position of joint action\" (PDF p. 426). No training study, injury data or force data are given: confidence `untested`. Inference: the \"even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods\" comparison (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415) puts these forms at or above the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods in intensity, so they belong after those methods in a progression, but the book gives no force data for the comparison ([[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]], 4.2.3). The book gives two power-load ranges six pages apart: \"about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411) and \"between 50-70 percent of one's 1RM\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416). Both differ from the Chapter 3 figures of about 25% of maximal force in isotonic laboratory models and about two-thirds of maximum in the competition squat ([[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]]); Jandacka and Vaverka 2008 put the acceleration-phase optimum at 47-63% 1RM by sex and exercise in physical-education students (55 men, 48 women) (vault brief, p. 11). Inference: treat 50-70% as a broad zone, not a fixed number."," "," **Example** — The author's own starting point: an accelerated-eccentric bench press or squat with \"about 40-50kg\" and very few repetitions (PDF p. 426 / printed p. 415). Inference: an actively accelerated ballistic bench at that load would be pulled down fast, stopped a few centimetres above the chest, and driven up at once."," ","-**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (band-accelerated descent), [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]], [[research-papers/jandacka-2008-a-regression-model-to-determine-load-for-maximum-power-output]], [[research-papers/handford-2024-a-conceptual-framework-of-different-eccentric-training-methods]] (maps accelerated eccentric loading among eccentric methods), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]","+**Related** — [[case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands]] (band-accelerated descent), [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]] (the 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods), [[framework--shock-method-and-plyometric-terminology]], [[claim--maximal-power-output-load-differs-lab-vs-competition-lift]], [[research-papers/jandacka-2008-a-regression-model-to-determine-load-for-maximum-power-output]], [[research-papers/handford-2024-a-conceptual-framework-of-different-eccentric-training-methods]] (maps accelerated eccentric loading among eccentric methods), [[framework--compensatory-acceleration-training]]"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d673ed2b-f7d1-4e3c-b987-89a0ed2ee173","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
