{"parentUuid":"760f0c7c-67c8-4b8e-954e-d829a46c82f6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015SVry3mEfoJJ91aZ5H6ra1","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"3f881841-e4e0-493b-a187-d409cc813ad8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:26:15.775Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 7.2-7.6 (Ch7b), 14 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch7b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 10 FIX rows, 7 numbered fix groups, 5 missing ideas).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 414-431 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f7b_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 415, 416, 417, 418, 422, 425, 426, 427, 428, 429 and 430 at 150 dpi and PDF 423 and 424 at 300 dpi (Figs 7.10-7.12) to `staging/_renders/f7b_p<N>.png`, plus a crop of the 7.4 opening (`f7b_p427_top.png`). I opened, as images: 415 (Knott and Voss, classical PNF), 416 (principle 4), 417 (CR repetitions), 418 (SRHR, gaze and compensation sentences), 423 (Table 7.1, Fig 7.10), 424 (Figs 7.11, 7.12, \"If we examine Figure 7.10\"), 425 (box squat items 1-4, \"may be used very effectively\", Fig 7.13), 426 (powermetrics, \"even more forceful\"), 427 top (\"brachialis and brachialis\"), 428 (7.4.1 spine and shoulder lists), 429 (elbow note, 7.4.2, \"the author ... (Siff, 1989)\"), 430 (\"acted as the basis\", \"the latter\", 7.5 methods list). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nAlso checked: the vault briefs `research-papers/frost-2010-a-biomechanical-evaluation-of-resistance.md` (line 49, p. 21), `research-papers/rhea-2009-...md` (lines 34-53, pp. 3-5) and `research-papers/jandacka-2008-...md` (line 34, p. 11).\nScope: the 14 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7b.txt`. I edited the 10 flagged entries. No GO entry was edited. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (14 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 7a or 6d file, or the vault.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| framework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system.md | **Fix 1.** The first sentence now reads: \"Section 7.2 is Siff's: in 7.5 the book says \"the author\" gave the 1989 NSCA presentation (Siff, 1989) (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418) and that \"An overview of this presentation and workshop acted as the basis for Chapter 7.2 above\" (PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419).\" \"he states ... his\" is gone. **Fix 2.** \"exercising of related muscle groups (principle 4)\" added to the movement-form group (PDF 416, verified on the image). **Missing idea 2** added to When to apply beside the dumbbell-curl pivot example: the 7.4 \"'bicep curls' do not involve the brachial biceps only, but also the brachialis and brachialis ...\" sentence (PDF 427 / p.416, doubled word printed so, seen on the crop) and the 7.4.1 elbow-flexion list (PDF 429 / p.418), with an Inference that the second \"brachialis\" is probably meant as brachioradialis. |\n| framework--pnf-activation-and-relaxation-techniques.md | **Fix 1** (shared parenthetical, below). **Fix 3.** The 7.5 sentence now reads: stretching is usually done \"in a static manner separately from the main exercises\", when its effectiveness \"would increase markedly if the latter [the main exercises] were also to be used statically and dynamically over a full range with progressively increasing resistance incorporating relevant PNF procedures\" (PDF 430 / p.419). When NOT now reads \"clinical techniques of classical PNF (the Knott and Voss approach, PDF p. 415 / printed p. 404), described here with repetition counts only (CR \"is repeated several times\" and \"continued gently for a few repetitions\", PDF p. 417; SRHR uses \"several repetitions\", PDF p. 418), and without loads, hold durations, rest periods or outcome data\". |\n| claim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md | **Fix 1.** **Missing idea 3** added to When to apply: \"Movement is promoted by the eyes following the extremities, or the extremities following the eyes,\" and \"Compensatory movements of additional muscle groups may be resisted or they may even be elicited by brief, sudden actions or deviations from optimal patterns\" (PDF 418 / p.407), with a labelled Inference and a link to the safety-factor entry. |\n| heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md | **Fix 1.** **Missing idea 4** added to When to apply: the 7.5 list of PNF methods to graft onto Iron Game training, quoted in full (pre-stretch ... \"cheating to compel stronger muscles to help the weaker ones\" ... commencement in the strongest range) with \"integrate very naturally into the extensive scheme of bodybuilding and weightlifting methods\" (PDF 430 / p.419), and the reverse claim that \"many physiotherapists\" did not know \"some of the bodybuilding and weightlifting methods could be valuable in the rehabilitation setting\" (PDF 429 / p.418). |\n| principle--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces.md | **Fix 1** only. |\n| claim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md | **Fix 1** only. The optional \"detrimental\" source note was not applied. |\n| case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md | **Fix 4.** `confidence: untested` is now `supported`. When NOT opens: \"One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate.\" (a) now \"about 2.3-2.4 times\" (306/129 = 2.37, 306/131 = 2.34) and \"about 345 against about 115-125\". (b) now locates Fig 7.10 at PDF 423 / p.412 and the \"near the beginning\" quote at PDF 424 / p.413; force start widened to \"about 165-170\", peak to \"about 225-230\". (c) now: rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, highest at about 370-375 near 85-90%: \"the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows.\" |\n| case-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md | **Fix 5.** The Frost sentence is replaced: \"Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone, so it is not what Frost tests. Frost's point is against the authors' claim that bands \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF p. 425 / printed p. 414): improved transfer was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference.\" Added: Frost's acute summary (bands may sustain late-phase force or velocity) does not contradict items 1-4. Rhea is now scoped to the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome, not items 1-4, with the brief's statistical power of .67 (p. 4), and an Inference that `contested` is scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim. The item 4 mechanism is now \"Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising as the lifter stands; the book does not state the mechanism.\" |\n| framework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md | **Fix 6.** \"in untrained students\" is now \"in physical-education students (55 men, 48 women)\". **Missing idea 5** added to What it is: the exercises can be done in a way \"which can be even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods discussed in Ch 4.2.3\" (PDF 426 / p.415), plus a labelled Inference in When NOT placing the four forms on that scale, linking [[framework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages]] (the 4.2.3 entry), also added to Related. |\n| claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md | **Fix 7.** When NOT now carries the review's sentence: 7.4.1 lists spine flexion as \"Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas\" (PDF 428 / p.417), without the obliques that 7.4.2 says also flex the trunk (PDF 429 / p.418), and its rotation line names only the obliques, not multifidus; \"the two sections do not fully agree\". Then an Inference on why `supported` is kept (see Disagreements 3). **Missing idea 1** added as a source note labelled \"(Inference, the entry writer's reading, not the book's)\": shoulder flexion \"Upwards in sagittal plane - posterior deltoid, upper trapezius\" (PDF 428 / p.417) reads as an error for the anterior deltoid; the elbow note \"Apparent rotation of the elbow joint is actually rotation at the wrist - the radius and ulna twist about one another\" (PDF 429 / p.418) names the wrist for a twist at the radioulnar joints. |\n\n**Fix 1, shared wording.** The five entries that carried \"(7.2 rests on his 1989 NSCA workshop, PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419)\" now carry \"(in 7.5 the book says \"the author\" gave the 1989 NSCA presentation (Siff, 1989), PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview of this presentation and workshop acted as the basis for Chapter 7.2 above\", PDF p. 430 / printed p. 419)\". Applied with a literal perl replacement; one hit per file.\n\n## Confidence changes\n\n| file | before | after | reason |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md | untested | supported | Table 7.1 is measured data on one group under three conditions (PDF 423), not an anecdote; the brief caps single-lab empirical findings at `supported`. Scoped in the entry to the descriptive table only; figure readings stay \"about\". |\n\nNo other rating changed. Box squat stays `contested` (now scoped to the effectiveness and transfer claim). Trunk stays `supported` (Disagreements 3).\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Errors in the 7.4.1 joint-action summary (PDF 428-429) | Source note in the trunk entry's When NOT, labelled as the writer's reading (Inference). Each error has its own locator: shoulder flexion PDF 428, elbow note PDF 429. |\n| 2 | \"Bicep curls\" are not biceps-only; \"brachialis and brachialis\" (PDF 427, 429) | PNF framework entry, When to apply, beside the dumbbell-curl pivot example. |\n| 3 | Gaze-limb coupling and deliberate deviation (PDF 418) | Novices entry, When to apply. That entry already quotes the surrounding 7.2.3.3 paragraph. |\n| 4 | 7.5 list of PNF methods for Iron Game training; physios can use Iron Game methods (PDF 429-430) | Pulleys entry, When to apply. |\n| 5 | Accelerated powermetrics \"even more forceful\" than Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch (PDF 426) | Powermetrics entry, What it is, plus an Inference in When NOT. |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch7b.txt` is unchanged (14 lines).\n\n## Frost 2010 and Rhea 2009: which book claim each bears on\n\n- **Frost 2010** (vault brief line 49, review p. 21): \"Bands may sustain acceleration and late-phase force or velocity, but ... improved transfer to sport had not been demonstrated and much acute evidence showed no difference from load-matched free weights.\" It bears on the authors' **effectiveness and transfer claim**, \"similar combinations of free weights and elastic bands may be used very effectively\" (PDF 425 / p.414). It does **not** bear against items 1-4: item 1 compares 200 kg plus 150-250 kg of band with 200 kg alone (not load-matched), and Frost's late-phase-force sentence is consistent with item 4.\n- **Rhea 2009** (vault brief lines 34-53: 48 NCAA D-I men, 16 per group, 12 weeks; pp. 4-5): jump power +17.8 / 11.0 / 4.8%; bands beat slow (p = .02), not fast-only (p = .09); strength n.s.; statistical power .67. It bears on the same **effectiveness claim**, as a training outcome. It does not bear on items 1-4, which are acute force measures, and it does not separate the bands from fast bar speed.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Fix 4(1) is half right.** Fig 7.10 is on PDF 423 / p.412, as the review says. But item (b)'s \"(PDF p. 424)\" was attached to the text quote \"near the beginning\", and that quote is on PDF 424 / p.413 (the \"If we examine Figure 7.10\" paragraph, seen on the image). So item (b) now carries both locators: the figure at 423 and the quote at 424. Item (a)'s locator for \"some three times greater\" was also narrowed to PDF 424.\n2. **Fix 3, CR repetitions.** The review gives \"a few\" for CR and \"several\" for SRHR. PDF 417 says CR \"is repeated several times\" and is \"continued gently for a few repetitions\". The entry quotes both for CR, and \"several repetitions\" for SRHR (PDF 418).\n3. **Fix 7: `contested` not set.** The two sections do address the same question (which muscles produce trunk flexion and rotation), so I recorded both with pages. But 7.4.1 is a list of movers and never says \"only\" or \"solely\"; 7.4.2 adds the obliques to flexion and multifidus to rotation rather than reversing anything. That is X against X-plus-more, not X against not-X, which is the brief's trigger for `contested`. The heel-raise entry in this batch records the same pattern (7.4.1 plantarflexion list without FHL, 7.4.2 adds it) under `supported`, and the review accepted that. The reasoning is labelled Inference in the entry.\n4. **Missing idea 1, elbow note.** The review says forearm rotation happens at the radioulnar joints. The book's note itself says \"the radius and ulna twist about one another\" (PDF 429), so the book does describe the radioulnar twist; the error is only the phrase \"at the wrist\". The source note is worded that way.\n5. **Fix 5, Frost quote.** Frost's late-phase-force point is paraphrased from the vault brief, not put in quote marks, because the brief is a summary, not Frost's text.\n6. **Optional items not applied.** The optional rows for GO entries (hypertrophy items 7 and 9 pointer, core-stability link) and the optional rows for flagged entries (Fig 7.5 caption oddity in the PNF framework, the \"detrimental\" source note in the functional entry, \"text layer\" wording) were not applied, following the ch5b precedent. The PNF framework's \"Quotes from PDF pp. 414-418 are from the text layer\" sentence stays, as the review allows; the new quotes I added from PDF 415-418 say \"checked against the page image\".\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: \"the author ... (Siff, 1989)\" on PDF 429 and \"acted as the basis for Chapter 7.2\" on PDF 430; principle 4 on PDF 416; \"the latter\" on PDF 430; Table 7.1 values and Fig 7.10 on PDF 423; Fig 7.12 force about 350 at 30%, slight mid dip, about 370-375 near 90%; Fig 7.10 power about 345, Figs 7.11-7.12 about 115-125; Jandacka \"Fifty-five male and 48 female physical-education students\" (brief line 34); 7.4.1 flexion and rotation lines on PDF 428.\n\n## Proof\n\nGrep for the new wording, one line per check (`file: [phrase] count`):\n\n```\nframework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system.md: [in 7.5 the book says \"the author\" gave the 1989 NSCA presentation (Siff, 1989) (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418)] 1\nframework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system.md: [exercising of related muscle groups (principle 4)] 1\nframework--pnf-five-p-factors-and-principles-as-a-conditioning-system.md: [brachialis and brachialis to differing degrees] 1\nframework--pnf-activation-and-relaxation-techniques.md: [if the latter [the main exercises] were also] 1\nframework--pnf-activation-and-relaxation-techniques.md: [with repetition counts only] 1\nframework--pnf-activation-and-relaxation-techniques.md: [classical PNF (the Knott and Voss approach, PDF p. 415] 1\nframework--pnf-activation-and-relaxation-techniques.md: [PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview] 1\nclaim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md: [PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview] 1\nheuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md: [PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview] 1\nprinciple--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces.md: [PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview] 1\nclaim--functional-training-means-sport-specific-and-no-exercise-is-non-functional.md: [PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview] 1\nclaim--novices-progress-from-mobility-to-stability-pnf-sequence.md: [eyes following the extremities] 1\nheuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns.md: [cheating to compel stronger muscles] 1\ncase-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md: [confidence: supported] 1\ncase-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md: [covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison] 1\ncase-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md: [Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412)] 1\ncase-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md: [about 2.3-2.4 times] 1\ncase-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md: [about 345 against about 115-125] 1\ncase-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md: [at most a faint shoulder at about 30%] 1\ncase-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md: [Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone] 1\ncase-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md: [Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising] 1\ncase-study--box-squat-with-free-weights-plus-elastic-bands.md: [bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome] 1\nframework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md: [physical-education students (55 men, 48 women)] 1\nframework--four-forms-of-accelerated-powermetrics.md: [even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods] 2\nclaim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md: [Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas] 1\nclaim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md: [Source note on the 7.4.1 summary (Inference] 1\n```\n\nOld phrases, count over all 14 scoped files (all 0; \"confidence: untested\" remains in 6 other 7b entries by design and is 0 in the Fradd file):\n\n```\n7.2 rests on his 1989: 0\nhe states that an overview of his: 0\nstretching would gain \"markedly\": 0\nclinical techniques from Knott and Voss: 0\nabout 2.4 times: 0\nin untrained students: 0\nThat is a named library source against: 0\nItem 4 follows from the band: 0\nis not visible in this printed curve: 0\nconfidence: untested (Fradd file): 0\n```\n\nMechanical greps over the 14 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash`, `F=$(tr -d \"\\r\" < staging/_scope_supertraining_ch7b.txt)`):\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== em dashes in titles:\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== header count per file:\n  14 6\n== CR endings:\n0\n== new link targets:\nprinciple--train-for-a-safety-factor-above-competition-forces.md\nheuristic--heel-raises-with-inversion-and-dorsiflexion-both-ways.md\nframework--kinetic-energy-stimulation-mechanical-basis-and-advantages.md\n== scope lines:\n      14\n```\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nNo 7b file is flagged. The one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside scope.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"760f0c7c-67c8-4b8e-954e-d829a46c82f6"