{"parentUuid":"44969469-10eb-46ac-a6d0-9c445d4b77a4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffrSMNmXx4VTS7N3vwQN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FLzUjJLy3ddv35kdjszCsc","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && perl -pi -e 's/\\Qwhile the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF p. 424 \\/ printed p. 413)\\E/while the text says \"some three times greater\" (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 \\/ printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424 \\/ printed p. 413)/' case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md && grep -c \"Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424\" case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md && perl -pi -e 's/\\QItem (a)'\"'\"'s locator for \"some three times greater\" was also narrowed to PDF 424.\\E/Item (a) now cites the table at PDF 423 and the \"some three times greater\" text at PDF 424./' /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md && grep -c \"Item (a) now cites\" /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7981,"cache_read_input_tokens":184596,"output_tokens":719,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":156},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7981},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":719,"cache_read_input_tokens":184596,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7981,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7981,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FLzUjJLy3ddv35kdjszCsc":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && perl -pi -e 's/\\Qwhile the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF p. 424 \\/ printed p. 413)\\E/while the text says \"some three times greater\" (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 \\/ printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424 \\/ printed p. 413)/' case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md && grep -c \"Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424\" case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md && perl -pi -e 's/\\QItem (a)'\"'\"'s locator for \"some three times greater\" was also narrowed to PDF 424.\\E/Item (a) now cites the table at PDF 423 and the \"some three times greater\" text at PDF 424./' /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md && grep -c \"Item (a) now cites\" /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md; cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffrSCFqiLcpeW8zBgwkK","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fe3cd08d-d579-48c9-898e-02608cc402f1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:26:24.281Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fe3cd08d-d579-48c9-898e-02608cc402f1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FLzUjJLy3ddv35kdjszCsc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\n1\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e21946e4-b056-4023-b0b8-f63ae0b8f350","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:26:24.994Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1\n1\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":47,"oldLines":7,"newStart":47,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," ## Disagreements with the review"," ","-1. **Fix 4(1) is half right.** Fig 7.10 is on PDF 423 / p.412, as the review says. But item (b)'s \"(PDF p. 424)\" was attached to the text quote \"near the beginning\", and that quote is on PDF 424 / p.413 (the \"If we examine Figure 7.10\" paragraph, seen on the image). So item (b) now carries both locators: the figure at 423 and the quote at 424. Item (a)'s locator for \"some three times greater\" was also narrowed to PDF 424.","+1. **Fix 4(1) is half right.** Fig 7.10 is on PDF 423 / p.412, as the review says. But item (b)'s \"(PDF p. 424)\" was attached to the text quote \"near the beginning\", and that quote is on PDF 424 / p.413 (the \"If we examine Figure 7.10\" paragraph, seen on the image). So item (b) now carries both locators: the figure at 423 and the quote at 424. Item (a) now cites the table at PDF 423 and the \"some three times greater\" text at PDF 424."," 2. **Fix 3, CR repetitions.** The review gives \"a few\" for CR and \"several\" for SRHR. PDF 417 says CR \"is repeated several times\" and is \"continued gently for a few repetitions\". The entry quotes both for CR, and \"several repetitions\" for SRHR (PDF 418)."," 3. **Fix 7: `contested` not set.** The two sections do address the same question (which muscles produce trunk flexion and rotation), so I recorded both with pages. But 7.4.1 is a list of movers and never says \"only\" or \"solely\"; 7.4.2 adds the obliques to flexion and multifidus to rotation rather than reversing anything. That is X against X-plus-more, not X against not-X, which is the brief's trigger for `contested`. The heel-raise entry in this batch records the same pattern (7.4.1 plantarflexion list without FHL, 7.4.2 adds it) under `supported`, and the review accepted that. The reasoning is labelled Inference in the entry."," 4. **Missing idea 1, elbow note.** The review says forearm rotation happens at the radioulnar joints. The book's note itself says \"the radius and ulna twist about one another\" (PDF 429), so the book does describe the radioulnar twist; the error is only the phrase \"at the wrist\". The source note is worded that way."]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":27,"oldLines":7,"newStart":27,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — When choosing a resistance mode for a goal: free weight for peak power, combined resistance for high force across the range. The authors' rule for all variable resistance: \"it is important to fully understand the emphasis of the resistance training being used, because frequent use of any inappropriate training method or resistance pattern can be detrimental to the performance of the actual sporting movements,\" and for maximal power, \"train regularly with loads that do not exceed about 60-70 percent of one's maximum\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411, checked against the page image). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work.\" (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides))."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.3-2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 345 against about 115-125 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412) starts at about 165-170 and peaks at about 225-230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413) rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, and is highest at about 370-375 near 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end: the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows. (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s.","+**When NOT to apply** — One single-joint movement, an unreported sample, a student project: `supported` covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison for this sample (free weight gives the highest velocity and power; isokinetic and combined give the highest force, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412), not any training-effect claim. Everything read from Figs 7.10-7.12 below is approximate. Four places where the section does not line up with itself, (a) to (c) read from the page images and (d) text against data: (a) Table 7.1 gives 306 W against 129 and 131 W, about 2.3-2.4 times, while the text says \"some three times greater\" (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). Inference: Figs 7.10-7.12 show peak power of about 345 against about 115-125 on the plotted scale, nearer three times, so the text may describe the figures rather than the table. (b) Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412) starts at about 165-170 and peaks at about 225-230 near 20-40% of displacement, then declines slowly; the text says peak force is \"near the beginning\" (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413). (c) Fig 7.12's combined force curve (PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413) rises to about 350 by about 30%, dips slightly through the middle, and is highest at about 370-375 near 85-90% before a sharp drop at the end: the curve shows at most a faint shoulder at about 30% and its main peak at about 85-90%; nothing peaks near the beginning, so finding 4's \"near the beginning and the end of range\" is not what this printed curve shows. (d) The 7.3 opening says greatest force is \"usually ... possible after the joint has passed through the midpoint of the movement\" (PDF p. 422 / printed p. 411), yet the free-weight data here put force highest early. Inference: the opening speaks of maximal joint strength across the range; Fig 7.10 shows force applied to a given accelerating load. Zatsiorsky describes ascending, descending and concave strength curves, with elbow flexion strongest at about 90 degrees ([[science-and-practice-of-strength-training--zatsiorsky/framework--joint-strength-curves-sticking-points-and-moment-arm]]), so \"after the midpoint\" is not a general rule. Velocity and acceleration in Figs 7.10-7.12 share one unlabelled axis with force and power, so their plotted heights cannot be read as m/s."," "," **Example** — The study itself: in the combined condition, mean force was 336 N against 184 N under free weight, while peak power was 131 W against 306 W (Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412, checked against the page image)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fe3cd08d-d579-48c9-898e-02608cc402f1","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
