_input_tokens":193676,"cache_creation_input_tokens":735,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":735,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016uUxDyEvaHFcPQrPFTDLjx":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky && perl -pi -e 's/so this entry records both and keeps `supported`\\./so this entry records both and rates the claim `supported`./' claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md && grep -c \"rates the claim \\`supported\\`\" claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md; grep -c \"keeps \\`supported\\`\" claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md; grep -c -F \"Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413\" case-study--fradd-free-weight-isokinetic-and-combined-elbow-flexion.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffrSsaxLeToySRQdop5J","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"a4f7ca50-8fc1-40e3-bfd2-c917072f5ff2","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:28:05.844Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"a4f7ca50-8fc1-40e3-bfd2-c917072f5ff2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"189515d2-55e8-482a-8b52-f518760b72bc","agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016uUxDyEvaHFcPQrPFTDLjx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\n0\n1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5fec6e90-3e21-4bbb-a83a-1ba7bed152ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:28:06.127Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1\n0\n1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — The authors' own scope is \"all sporting activities that require rotation of the trunk\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418). Inference: for throwers, batters and linemen who drive and turn, load rotation through the obliques rather than relying on back extensions, and when trunk flexion work is crunches only, add oblique-dominant patterns. Inference: the PNF trunk rotation pattern of Fig 7.8, done supine or standing with cable resistance, is the book's own tool for this (PDF p. 420 / printed p. 409, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--use-pulleys-and-dumbbells-for-spiral-diagonal-pnf-patterns]])."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The claim that multifidus is \"the only muscle of the back\" involved in spinal rotation is stated without citation. Inference: check this sentence against an anatomy reference before teaching it as exact. Claim (4) is given without data. Before any multi-joint analysis the authors themselves tell the reader to remember the end of Chapter 3: single-joint labels miss that \"muscles which do not span other joints can still produce acceleration about those joints\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416; [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]]). The book's own 7.4.1 summary lists spine flexion as \"Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), without the obliques that 7.4.2 says also flex the trunk (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418), and its rotation line names only the obliques, not multifidus; the two sections do not fully agree. Inference: 7.4.1 is a list of movers that leaves muscles out rather than saying \"only\" these muscles act, and 7.4.2 adds to it rather than reversing it, so this entry records both and keeps `supported`. Source note on the 7.4.1 summary (Inference, the entry writer's reading, not the book's): the summary that this entry and [[heuristic--heel-raises-with-inversion-and-dorsiflexion-both-ways]] rest on has reference-list errors. Shoulder flexion \"Upwards in sagittal plane\" is given as \"posterior deltoid, upper trapezius (for scapular rotation)\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), which reads as an error for the anterior deltoid. The elbow note says \"Apparent rotation of the elbow joint is actually rotation at the wrist - the radius and ulna twist about one another\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418); the twisting it describes is at the radioulnar joints, so \"at the wrist\" is the wrong place name. Check any muscle list from 7.4.1 against an anatomy reference before teaching it. Confidence `supported` covers the broad joint-action claims, not the multifidus sentence.","+**When NOT to apply** — The claim that multifidus is \"the only muscle of the back\" involved in spinal rotation is stated without citation. Inference: check this sentence against an anatomy reference before teaching it as exact. Claim (4) is given without data. Before any multi-joint analysis the authors themselves tell the reader to remember the end of Chapter 3: single-joint labels miss that \"muscles which do not span other joints can still produce acceleration about those joints\" (PDF p. 427 / printed p. 416; [[claim--multiarticular-muscle-action-cannot-be-predicted-from-joints-spanned]]). The book's own 7.4.1 summary lists spine flexion as \"Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), without the obliques that 7.4.2 says also flex the trunk (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418), and its rotation line names only the obliques, not multifidus; the two sections do not fully agree. Inference: 7.4.1 is a list of movers that leaves muscles out rather than saying \"only\" these muscles act, and 7.4.2 adds to it rather than reversing it, so this entry records both and rates the claim `supported`. Source note on the 7.4.1 summary (Inference, the entry writer's reading, not the book's): the summary that this entry and [[heuristic--heel-raises-with-inversion-and-dorsiflexion-both-ways]] rest on has reference-list errors. Shoulder flexion \"Upwards in sagittal plane\" is given as \"posterior deltoid, upper trapezius (for scapular rotation)\" (PDF p. 428 / printed p. 417), which reads as an error for the anterior deltoid. The elbow note says \"Apparent rotation of the elbow joint is actually rotation at the wrist - the radius and ulna twist about one another\" (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418); the twisting it describes is at the radioulnar joints, so \"at the wrist\" is the wrong place name. Check any muscle list from 7.4.1 against an anatomy reference before teaching it. Confidence `supported` covers the broad joint-action claims, not the multifidus sentence."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: sit-ups done with a lateral flex shift work from rectus abdominis to the obliques and psoas (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--trunk-rotation-is-driven-by-the-obliques-not-the-erector-spinae.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a4f7ca50-8fc1-40e3-bfd2-c917072f5ff2","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
