{"parentUuid":"816c2e72-511a-4387-b54a-f5ce6cc207da","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"afcd6d73916c3b3e0","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffrb6VKrbNRNG22Hky37","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All seven review fixes are applied, and the lint shows no issues in any 7b file. I checked each fix on the page images before making it. Fix 3 and Fix 4 were only partly right in the review, so I applied corrected versions. I edited only the 10 flagged 7b entries. The 4 GO entries, the 7a and 6d files, `_book.md` and the vault are untouched. No renames, no git.\n\nLog: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch7b_applied.md`\n\n**Per-fix outcome**\n1. **Siff attribution (applied, 6 files):** all six 7.2 entries now cite PDF 429 / p.418 for \"the author\" giving the 1989 NSCA talk (Siff, 1989), and PDF 430 / p.419 for \"acted as the basis for Chapter 7.2 above\". The PNF framework entry no longer says \"he states ... his\".\n2. **Principle 4 (applied):** \"exercising of related muscle groups (principle 4)\" is now in the PNF framework entry's movement-form group.\n3. **Activation entry (applied, wording corrected):** \"the latter [the main exercises]\" is fixed, and the Knott and Voss link is now to classical PNF (PDF 415). The review gave \"a few\" for contract-relax and \"several\" for slow-reversal-hold-relax. The page (PDF 417) gives contract-relax both: \"repeated several times\" and \"a few repetitions\". The entry now quotes both.\n4. **Fradd (applied, partly disagreed):** Fig 7.10 is on PDF 423, as the review says. But the \"near the beginning\" quote really is on PDF 424, so item (b) now carries both locators. The ratio reads \"about 2.3-2.4 times\" (306/129 = 2.37, 306/131 = 2.34), the plotted peaks read \"about 345 against about 115-125\", and the Fig 7.12 wording now says \"at most a faint shoulder at about 30%\".\n5. **Box squat (applied):** the Frost point now targets the effectiveness claim, not item 1. The item 4 mechanism is labelled \"Inference:\".\n6. **Powermetrics (applied):** \"physical-education students (55 men, 48 women)\", checked against the Jandacka vault brief, line 34.\n7. **Trunk (applied):** both pages are recorded (7.4.1 flexion and rotation lines on PDF 428; 7.4.2 on PDF 429). I did not set `contested`. Both sections answer the same question, but 7.4.1 is a list that leaves muscles out without saying \"only\", and 7.4.2 adds to it rather than reversing it. The heel-raise entry already handles the same pattern as `supported`. The reasoning is in the entry, labelled Inference.\n\n**Frost and Rhea against the book's claims (Fix 5)**\n- **Frost 2010** (vault brief, p.21) bears only on \"may be used very effectively\" (PDF 425): transfer to sport was not shown, and acute studies against load-matched free weights mostly found no difference. It does not test item 1, because 200 kg plus bands against 200 kg is not load-matched. Its point that bands may keep late-range force up is consistent with item 4.\n- **Rhea 2009** (vault brief, pp.4-5) bears on the same effectiveness claim, as a 12-week training result. Jump power rose 17.8% with bands, 11.0% fast-only and 4.8% slow; bands beat slow (p = .02) but not fast-only (p = .09), and strength did not differ. Statistical power was .67. It says nothing about the acute force items 1-4, and it cannot separate the bands from fast bar speed.\n- `contested` stays, limited to the effectiveness and transfer claim.\n\n**Confidence changes:** Fradd only, `untested` to `supported`. The entry limits that rating to the measured Table 7.1 comparison; every number read from the figures still says \"about\", and the entry makes no training-effect claim. Nothing else changed.\n\n**Missing ideas (no new entry; scope file still 14 lines)**\n\n| # | Idea | Where it went |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Errors in the 7.4.1 joint-action list | Trunk entry, When NOT, labelled as the writer's reading (Inference). Shoulder flexion \"posterior deltoid\" (PDF 428). Elbow note \"at the wrist\" (PDF 429): the book does mention the radius and ulna twisting, so only the place name is wrong. |\n| 2 | \"Bicep curls\" are not biceps-only (\"brachialis and brachialis\", PDF 427; elbow-flexion list, PDF 429) | PNF framework entry, beside the dumbbell-curl example |\n| 3 | Eyes leading the limbs, and deliberately resisting or provoking compensatory movements (PDF 418) | Novices entry, When to apply |\n| 4 | 7.5 list of PNF methods to add to gym training; physios using gym methods (PDF 429-430) | Pulleys entry, When to apply |\n| 5 | \"Even more forceful than\" the Ch 4.2.3 drop-and-catch methods (PDF 426) | Powermetrics entry, linked to the 4.2.3 kinetic-energy entry |\n\nI skipped the review's optional items, following the ch5b precedent. The log lists them.\n\n**Grep proof** (each count = matches in that file; the log also has extra rows for two late edits and the old-phrase checks)\n```\npnf-five-p-factors: [\"the author\" gave the 1989 NSCA presentation (Siff, 1989) (PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418)] 1\npnf-five-p-factors: [exercising of related muscle groups (principle 4)] 1 | [brachialis and brachialis to differing degrees] 1\npnf-activation: [if the latter [the main exercises] were also] 1 | [with repetition counts only] 1 | [classical PNF (the Knott and Voss approach, PDF p. 415] 1\n[PDF p. 429 / printed p. 418, and that \"An overview] 1 in each of: activation, novices, pulleys, safety-factor, functional\nnovices: [eyes following the extremities] 1 | pulleys: [cheating to compel stronger muscles] 1\nfradd: [confidence: supported] 1 | [covers only the descriptive Table 7.1 comparison] 1 | [Fig 7.10's force curve (PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412)] 1\nfradd: [about 2.3-2.4 times] 1 | [about 345 against about 115-125] 1 | [at most a faint shoulder at about 30%] 1\nfradd: [Table 7.1, PDF p. 423 / printed p. 412; text, PDF p. 424 / printed p. 413] 1\nbox-squat: [Item 1 compares a heavier total load with free weight alone] 1 | [Inference: item 4 follows from band tension rising] 1 | [bears on the same effectiveness claim as a training outcome] 1\npowermetrics: [physical-education students (55 men, 48 women)] 1 | [even more forceful than the load dropping and catching methods] 2\ntrunk: [Bilateral action of rectus abdominis and psoas] 1 | [Source note on the 7.4.1 summary (Inference] 1 | [rates the claim `supported`] 1\nOld phrases: all 0 across the 14 files (\"confidence: untested\" is 0 in the Fradd file)\nMechanical checks (14 files): no em dashes in titles or body, no review-history words, no wrapped wikilinks, all 14 have the 6 section headers, no CR line endings\n```\n\n**Lint**\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 152, 153]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\nThe only flag is the known false positive in `_book.md`, which I did not touch. Its line numbers differ from ch5b's because `_book.md` has changed since that run, not because of anything in this batch."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":505,"cache_read_input_tokens":194980,"output_tokens":2755,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":505},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2755,"cache_read_input_tokens":194980,"cache_creation_input_tokens":505,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":505,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffrauHdiCuf6pBSwB9VW","attributionAgent":"general-purp